DO $$
DECLARE
  v_cid UUID;
  v_ch  UUID;
BEGIN
  SELECT id INTO v_cid FROM public.courses
    WHERE title = 'AI Cybersecurity, Governance & Enterprise Compliance'
      AND curriculum_version = 'cyber-v1';
  IF v_cid IS NULL THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Cyber course not found — run structure migration first'; END IF;

-- ─── MODULE 1: Cybersecurity Foundations — CIA Triad & Zero Trust ────────────

  SELECT id INTO v_ch FROM public.chapters
    WHERE course_id = v_cid ORDER BY order_index LIMIT 1 OFFSET 0;
  IF v_ch IS NULL THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'M01 chapter not found'; END IF;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Master the CIA Triad — the foundational model behind every security decision ever made — and learn to apply it across enterprise systems, cloud environments, and AI platforms.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'CIA Triad — Confidentiality, Integrity & Availability',
      'description', 'Master the CIA Triad — the foundational model behind every security decision ever made — and learn to apply it across enterprise systems, cloud environments, and AI platforms.',
      'transcript', 'Every security decision you will ever make traces back to three properties: Confidentiality, Integrity, and Availability. This is the CIA Triad — the most durable framework in information security, first formalized in the 1970s and still the universal benchmark for evaluating security controls today.

Confidentiality means protecting information from unauthorized disclosure. It answers the question: who is allowed to see this? The mechanisms that enforce confidentiality include encryption, access controls, need-to-know permissions, and data classification policies. When a healthcare organization encrypts patient records with AES-256 and restricts database access to authorized clinical staff only, every one of those controls is a confidentiality measure. Breaches of confidentiality — data leaks, credential theft, insider threats — make headlines precisely because they are the most visible security failures.

Integrity means protecting information from unauthorized modification. It answers the question: can I trust that this data has not been altered? Integrity controls include cryptographic hashing (SHA-256, SHA-3), digital signatures, version control, write-once storage for audit logs, and database transaction controls. When a financial institution uses HMAC to verify that a payment instruction has not been tampered with in transit, that is an integrity control. Integrity matters most in high-stakes decision systems: medical dosing software, financial transaction processing, and increasingly, AI model outputs.

Availability means ensuring systems are accessible to authorized users when needed. It answers the question: is this system operational when the business requires it? Availability controls include redundant architecture, disaster recovery plans, DDoS mitigation, load balancing, and backup and recovery testing. A ransomware attack is simultaneously a confidentiality breach (data exfiltrated before encryption) and an availability failure (systems inaccessible until ransom paid or restoration completed).

The CIA Triad creates tension that you will manage your entire career. Maximum confidentiality — encrypting everything, restricting all access — reduces availability and increases operational friction. Maximum availability — making everything accessible everywhere — weakens confidentiality and integrity. Security architecture is the art of balancing these three properties according to the risk tolerance and business requirements of your organization.

Apply this framework to every security decision you face. A new vendor wants API access to your production database: which CIA properties are at risk? Your organization is moving to a SaaS platform: what does the shared responsibility model mean for each of the three properties? An AI model is being trained on customer data: what confidentiality and integrity controls are required for the training pipeline? The CIA Triad gives you a structured lens for answering every one of these questions.

In regulated environments, regulators think in CIA terms even when they do not use the acronym. HIPAA''s Security Rule safeguards map directly to CIA: confidentiality safeguards (access controls, encryption), integrity safeguards (audit controls, transmission security), and availability safeguards (contingency planning). SOC 2''s Trust Service Criteria — particularly the Security criterion — is organized around the same principles. NIST SP 800-53 control families align to CIA properties. Master this triad and you have the conceptual key to every compliance framework you will encounter.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 1;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Zero Trust has replaced the perimeter model as the architecture of modern enterprise security. Learn the five pillars, implementation strategy, and why identity is now the new perimeter.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Zero Trust Architecture — Never Trust, Always Verify',
      'description', 'Zero Trust has replaced the perimeter model as the architecture of modern enterprise security. Learn the five pillars, implementation strategy, and why identity is now the new perimeter.',
      'transcript', 'For forty years, enterprise security was built on the castle-and-moat model: build a strong perimeter around your network, trust everything inside it, and deny everything outside. This model collapsed when cloud computing, remote work, and mobile devices dissolved the perimeter entirely. Zero Trust is the replacement architecture, and it is now the foundational design principle for every serious enterprise security program.

The Zero Trust principle is deceptively simple: never trust, always verify. Every access request — regardless of whether it comes from inside the corporate network, a home office, a data center, or a cloud environment — must be explicitly authenticated and authorized. The origin of a request is not a signal of trustworthiness. A compromised machine inside your network is more dangerous than an authenticated request from outside it.

Zero Trust has five implementation pillars. The first is Identity: every user, service account, and device must have a verified identity, and authentication must be strong — meaning phishing-resistant MFA, not just a password. The second is Device: access decisions must factor in device health. A laptop without EDR installed, missing security patches, or flagged by MDM as non-compliant should not receive the same access as a healthy, managed device. The third is Network: traffic should be micro-segmented so that a breach in one segment cannot move laterally to others. Replace flat networks with software-defined perimeters and strict east-west traffic controls. The fourth is Application: applications should be accessed via identity-aware proxies, not exposed directly to the network. ZTNA (Zero Trust Network Access) replaces VPN as the access mechanism. The fifth is Data: classify data and apply access controls at the data layer, not just the network layer.

NIST SP 800-207 is the definitive Zero Trust Architecture standard. It defines seven tenets of Zero Trust and two reference architectures: identity-based (all access decisions flow through an Identity Provider) and network-based (software-defined perimeters control traffic flows). The Policy Engine is the decision point: it evaluates identity, device posture, and context to grant or deny access. The Policy Enforcement Point is the mechanism that enforces those decisions.

Implementation is incremental, not a big-bang replacement. The most common starting point is Identity: enforce MFA universally, implement Conditional Access policies, and audit service account privileges. This delivers the highest risk reduction per unit of implementation effort. Cloud environments are naturally Zero Trust-aligned — AWS IAM policies, Azure Conditional Access, and GCP IAM are all Zero Trust mechanisms at the application and identity layer.

The business case for Zero Trust is supported by breach data. The average cost of a data breach in 2025 exceeds $4.8M. The most common initial access vectors — phishing (leading to credential theft) and external remote services (VPN exploitation) — are both mitigated by Zero Trust identity and network controls. Organizations that have implemented mature Zero Trust architectures report significantly lower breach costs and dwell times. Zero Trust is not a product you buy. It is an architectural philosophy you implement across identity, device, network, application, and data layers systematically over time.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 2;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'IAM misconfigurations are the number one cloud breach vector. Master authentication, authorization, RBAC vs ABAC, MFA, and the principle of least privilege from first principles.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Identity & Access Management (IAM) Fundamentals',
      'description', 'IAM misconfigurations are the number one cloud breach vector. Master authentication, authorization, RBAC vs ABAC, MFA, and the principle of least privilege from first principles.',
      'transcript', 'Identity and Access Management is the discipline of ensuring the right people have the right access to the right resources at the right time — and that unauthorized access is prevented, detected, and logged. In a Zero Trust world, IAM is not a supporting security function; it is the primary security perimeter.

Start with the fundamental distinction between authentication and authorization. Authentication answers the question: are you who you claim to be? It is the process of verifying identity — through something you know (password), something you have (MFA token, hardware key), or something you are (biometric). Authorization answers: what are you allowed to do? Once identity is verified, the authorization system determines which resources the authenticated entity can access and with what permissions.

Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) is the single highest-impact security control available to most organizations. Enabling MFA blocks over 99% of automated account compromise attacks. But not all MFA is equal. SMS-based MFA is vulnerable to SIM-swapping attacks. TOTP (Time-based One-Time Password) apps like Google Authenticator are resistant to SIM-swapping but still phishable. FIDO2/WebAuthn hardware security keys (YubiKey, Titan Key) and passkeys are phishing-resistant — the highest available assurance level for most enterprise use cases.

Access control models define how authorization decisions are made. RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) assigns permissions to roles, and users are assigned to roles. Simpler to administer at scale, RBAC works well when access needs map cleanly to job functions. ABAC (Attribute-Based Access Control) makes authorization decisions based on attributes: user department, device type, time of day, data classification, and location. More granular and flexible than RBAC, ABAC enables Zero Trust conditional access policies but requires a mature policy engine to manage effectively.

The principle of least privilege (PoLP) mandates that every user, service account, and system should have only the minimum permissions required to perform their function — nothing more. Implementing PoLP requires access reviews (are current permissions still appropriate?), just-in-time (JIT) access (grant elevated privileges only when needed, revoke immediately after), and privilege analytics (which accounts have privileges they never use?). In AWS, this means avoiding wildcard (*) IAM policies. In Active Directory, it means removing users from Domain Admins when domain admin rights are not their daily job function.

Service accounts are a persistent security liability. Every application that needs to call an API, query a database, or run a scheduled task creates a service account. Service accounts frequently accumulate excessive permissions, use static passwords that are never rotated, and are shared across multiple applications — creating single-point-of-failure credential exposure. Modern best practice: use dynamic credentials (AWS IAM roles, GCP service accounts, Azure Managed Identities) that are assumed at runtime rather than stored as static credentials.

IAM in cloud environments is uniquely powerful and uniquely dangerous. AWS Identity Center, Azure Entra ID, and GCP IAM provide granular, programmable access control — but misconfigurations (open S3 buckets, overpermissive service accounts, unused access keys) are responsible for the majority of cloud data breaches. Regular IAM access reviews using tools like AWS IAM Access Analyzer, Microsoft Entra Identity Governance, or third-party products like Varonis and Saviynt are essential for maintaining least-privilege posture at scale.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 3;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Cryptography is the mathematical backbone of all digital security. Master symmetric and asymmetric encryption, hashing, digital signatures, TLS 1.3, and the PKI chain of trust.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Encryption, Cryptography & Public Key Infrastructure (PKI)',
      'description', 'Cryptography is the mathematical backbone of all digital security. Master symmetric and asymmetric encryption, hashing, digital signatures, TLS 1.3, and the PKI chain of trust.',
      'transcript', 'Cryptography is the mathematical foundation of every secure communication, every digital signature, every protected database, and every authenticated web session. Understanding cryptography at a working level — not as a mathematician, but as a security professional — is non-negotiable.

Symmetric encryption uses the same key to encrypt and decrypt. AES (Advanced Encryption Standard) is the global standard: AES-128 and AES-256 are both considered computationally secure for practical purposes, with AES-256 preferred for data that needs to remain secure for decades. The limitation of symmetric encryption is the key distribution problem: how do you securely share the key with the other party? If you could already communicate securely, you would not need encryption in the first place.

Asymmetric encryption solves the key distribution problem using mathematically linked key pairs. Each party has a public key (shared freely) and a private key (never shared). Data encrypted with a public key can only be decrypted with the corresponding private key. RSA (2048-bit or 4096-bit) and ECC (Elliptic Curve Cryptography — 256-bit provides equivalent security to RSA-3072 at much smaller key sizes) are the primary asymmetric algorithms. Asymmetric encryption is computationally expensive at scale, so in practice TLS uses asymmetric encryption to exchange a symmetric session key, then switches to AES for the bulk data transfer.

Cryptographic hashing produces a fixed-size digest from arbitrary-length input. Good hash functions are one-way (cannot reverse from hash to input) and collision-resistant (cannot find two inputs that produce the same hash). SHA-256 and SHA-3 are the current standards. MD5 and SHA-1 are cryptographically broken — do not use them for security purposes. Hashing use cases: password storage (with salting and using bcrypt/Argon2/scrypt, not raw SHA-256), file integrity verification, digital signatures, and blockchain.

Digital signatures use asymmetric cryptography to provide authentication and non-repudiation. To sign a document: hash the document, then encrypt the hash with your private key. The recipient decrypts the hash with your public key and compares it to their own hash of the document. If they match, the signature is valid — proving the document came from you (authentication) and has not been modified (integrity). Code signing certificates on software packages use exactly this mechanism.

PKI (Public Key Infrastructure) is the system of trust that validates public keys belong to who they claim. The chain of trust: a Root CA (Certificate Authority) that is inherently trusted (pre-installed in operating systems and browsers) signs Intermediate CA certificates, which sign End Entity certificates (the HTTPS certificate on your website). Certificate fields: Subject (who the cert belongs to), Issuer (who signed it), validity period, Subject Alternative Names (SANs), and the public key. Certificate revocation: CRL (Certificate Revocation List — a published list of revoked certs) and OCSP (Online Certificate Status Protocol — real-time revocation check). TLS 1.3 is the current standard: eliminated weak cipher suites, removed RSA key exchange (now requires forward secrecy via ECDHE), and reduced handshake latency from two round-trips to one. Never allow TLS 1.0 or 1.1 in production environments.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 4;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Threat modeling identifies security risks before they become incidents. Master STRIDE, attack trees, PASTA methodology, and mapping threats to NIST CSF 2.0 controls.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Threat Modeling & NIST Security Control Frameworks',
      'description', 'Threat modeling identifies security risks before they become incidents. Master STRIDE, attack trees, PASTA methodology, and mapping threats to NIST CSF 2.0 controls.',
      'transcript', 'Threat modeling is the practice of systematically identifying what could go wrong with a system, why an attacker would want to exploit it, and what controls would prevent or detect those attacks. Done well, threat modeling is the highest-return security investment you can make — because it is always cheaper to design security in than to bolt it on after a breach.

STRIDE is the most widely used threat modeling framework, originally developed at Microsoft. Each letter represents a threat category: Spoofing (impersonating another user or system — attacks authentication), Tampering (modifying data or code in transit or at rest — attacks integrity), Repudiation (denying you performed an action — attacks non-repudiation), Information Disclosure (exposing data to unauthorized parties — attacks confidentiality), Denial of Service (making a system unavailable — attacks availability), and Elevation of Privilege (gaining more permissions than intended — attacks authorization).

Apply STRIDE to a data flow diagram (DFD) of your system. Draw every component — users, applications, databases, APIs, third-party services — and every data flow between them. Then systematically ask: which STRIDE threats apply to each component and each data flow? A web application has a login endpoint: Spoofing (credential theft), Tampering (request manipulation), Repudiation (no audit log), Information Disclosure (verbose error messages), DoS (no rate limiting), and EoP (SQL injection to bypass auth). This exercise surfaces threats methodically, ensuring you do not rely on guesswork or the individual experience of any single engineer.

Attack trees model threats hierarchically. The root node is the attacker''s goal (e.g., "Steal customer PII"). Child nodes are the methods to achieve that goal (SQL injection, insider access, vendor compromise). Each child can have its own children — sub-methods and prerequisites. Attack trees are particularly useful for quantitative risk analysis: assign a cost or probability to each leaf node and roll them up to the root to estimate overall exploit likelihood and cost.

PASTA (Process for Attack Simulation and Threat Analysis) is a seven-stage risk-centric methodology: Define Objectives → Define Technical Scope → Decompose Application → Threat Analysis → Vulnerability & Weaknesses Analysis → Attack Modeling → Risk & Impact Analysis. PASTA aligns threat modeling to business risk — threats are scored not just by technical severity but by business impact. It is favored in enterprise environments where security decisions must be justified in financial terms.

NIST CSF 2.0 (Cybersecurity Framework, updated 2024) adds a sixth function to the original five. The framework now covers: Govern (new — establish cybersecurity strategy, policies, and accountability), Identify (understand assets, risks, and gaps), Protect (deploy safeguards), Detect (implement monitoring and detection), Respond (contain and eradicate incidents), and Recover (restore operations). Map every control you implement to a CSF function. NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5 provides the full control catalog (20 control families, 1,000+ controls) that CSF functions point to. For AI systems, NIST AI RMF (AI Risk Management Framework) maps directly to CSF''s Govern-Identify-Protect-Detect-Respond-Recover model.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 5;

-- ─── MODULE 2: Enterprise Security Operations — SOC, SIEM & XDR ─────────────

  SELECT id INTO v_ch FROM public.chapters
    WHERE course_id = v_cid ORDER BY order_index LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1;
  IF v_ch IS NULL THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'M02 chapter not found'; END IF;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'The SOC is the operational heart of enterprise security. Learn how Tier 1–3 analyst roles work, the full SOC technology stack, and the metrics that define SOC effectiveness.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Security Operations Center (SOC) — Structure, Roles & Tiers',
      'description', 'The SOC is the operational heart of enterprise security. Learn how Tier 1–3 analyst roles work, the full SOC technology stack, and the metrics that define SOC effectiveness.',
      'transcript', 'The Security Operations Center is the 24/7 nerve center of enterprise security — the team responsible for monitoring for threats, detecting incidents, and coordinating the organization''s response. Understanding SOC structure is essential whether you are building one, staffing one, managing one, or doing compliance work that depends on its effectiveness.

SOC staffing is organized into tiers that reflect escalating expertise. Tier 1 analysts are the first line: they monitor the SIEM dashboard, triage incoming alerts, and apply playbooks to classify events as true positives or false positives. Tier 1 work is high-volume and repetitive — the goal is speed and consistency, not deep investigation. Tier 2 analysts investigate confirmed incidents: they correlate events across multiple data sources, determine scope and impact, execute containment procedures, and escalate to Tier 3 when needed. Tier 3 analysts handle the hardest cases: advanced threat hunting, malware analysis, zero-day investigation, and digital forensics. Tier 3 analysts also build the detection rules and playbooks that Tier 1 and Tier 2 execute.

The SOC technology stack has four pillars. SIEM (Security Information and Event Management) is the brain: it ingests logs from across the environment, correlates events to surface alerts, and provides the investigation interface. SOAR (Security Orchestration, Automation & Response) is the muscle: it automates repetitive analyst tasks, runs enrichment playbooks, and coordinates response actions. EDR (Endpoint Detection & Response) provides deep visibility into endpoint behavior — process execution, file writes, network connections, and memory. UEBA (User and Entity Behavior Analytics) detects anomalous behavior patterns that rules-based detection misses — the compromised account that logs in at 3 AM and downloads 50GB of data.

SOC coverage models range from fully in-house to fully outsourced. Fully in-house: maximum control, highest cost, requires 24/7 staff. Managed SOC / MSSP: vendor provides Tier 1 and Tier 2 monitoring, organization retains Tier 3 and response authority. MDR (Managed Detection & Response): premium managed service that includes active threat hunting and incident response, not just monitoring. Co-managed: organization staffs Tier 2 and Tier 3, MSSP provides Tier 1 coverage for nights and weekends. The right model depends on risk appetite, budget, and the sensitivity of the environment.

SOC effectiveness is measured quantitatively. MTTD (Mean Time to Detect): the average time from when a threat enters the environment to when the SOC detects it. The industry median MTTD for advanced threats is still measured in days — best-in-class SOCs measure it in hours. MTTR (Mean Time to Respond): time from detection to containment. False positive rate: the percentage of alerts that turn out to be non-incidents. A false positive rate above 50% is an analyst burnout risk — high-noise environments cause alert fatigue, which causes real threats to be dismissed. Alert-to-analyst ratio: how many alerts does each analyst handle per shift? More than 100 alerts per analyst per day is unsustainable without significant automation.

SOC maturity follows a progression. Level 1 (Initial): alerts acknowledged but not systematically investigated. Level 2 (Developing): formal incident classification and escalation procedures. Level 3 (Defined): documented playbooks, formal IR process, metrics tracked. Level 4 (Advanced): proactive threat hunting, continuous detection engineering, automation deployed. Level 5 (Optimizing): continuous improvement loops, AI-augmented detection, threat intelligence integrated at every tier. Most organizations operate at Level 2 or 3 — the gap to Level 4 is almost always about people and process, not technology.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 1;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'SIEM is the brain of the SOC. Master log ingestion, correlation rule writing, alert triage methodology, and the platforms that power enterprise threat detection.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'SIEM Mastery — Log Ingestion, Correlation Rules & Alert Triage',
      'description', 'SIEM is the brain of the SOC. Master log ingestion, correlation rule writing, alert triage methodology, and the platforms that power enterprise threat detection.',
      'transcript', 'SIEM — Security Information and Event Management — is the platform that makes enterprise security monitoring possible at scale. It ingests logs from every system in the environment, normalizes them into a common format, correlates events across sources, and surfaces alerts when suspicious patterns emerge. Without SIEM, a SOC analyst looking for a threat would need to manually review logs across hundreds of systems simultaneously — an impossible task. With SIEM, threats leave traces that correlation rules surface automatically.

Log sources are the foundation. Every SIEM deployment starts with source coverage: which systems are sending logs, and are they sending the right events? Critical sources: firewalls and network appliances (connection logs, denied traffic), Active Directory and identity providers (authentication events, group membership changes, privileged access), endpoints via EDR or Windows Event Forwarding (process creation, network connections, file writes), cloud platforms (AWS CloudTrail, Azure Activity Log, GCP Audit Logs), web application firewalls, email security gateways, and VPN access logs. Log completeness directly determines detection coverage — a blind spot in log coverage is a blind spot in detection.

Log normalization converts heterogeneous log formats into a common schema. Microsoft Sentinel uses the Advanced Security Information Model (ASIM). Splunk uses Common Information Model (CIM). Elastic uses Elastic Common Schema (ECS). Normalization enables cross-source correlation rules — you can write a rule that joins a Windows authentication event with a network flow event and a VPN login event without worrying about the different field names each source uses.

Correlation rules define what the SIEM should alert on. A simple rule: alert when five or more failed login attempts occur for the same account within two minutes (brute force detection). A complex rule: alert when a user authenticates from a geographic location different from their previous 30 days of logins, within 30 minutes of an authentication from their normal location (impossible travel, indicating credential theft). Rule quality determines SOC performance. Well-tuned rules with low false positive rates make analysts effective. Poorly tuned rules generate noise, cause alert fatigue, and result in real threats being dismissed.

Alert triage is a structured skill, not intuition. When a SIEM alert fires, the analyst asks: Is this alert technically valid (did the SIEM correctly identify the described event, or is this a parsing error)? Is this alert contextually suspicious (is this event unusual for this user/system/time, or is it expected behavior)? What is the potential impact if this is malicious (which assets are involved, what data could be exposed)? What additional evidence supports or refutes malicious intent? This triage framework converts raw alerts into classified incidents — true positive (real threat), false positive (benign event), or false positive with tuning recommendation (rule needs refinement).

Major SIEM platforms each have distinct strengths. Microsoft Sentinel: cloud-native, tight Azure and M365 integration, consumption-based pricing model, best for Microsoft-heavy environments. Splunk Enterprise Security: most powerful search language (SPL), extensive ecosystem, high licensing cost — preferred by large enterprises with complex environments. Elastic SIEM: open-source core with commercial add-ons, excellent for high-volume log environments, requires more operational expertise. Wazuh: fully open-source, strong for compliance-focused small to mid-size deployments. Chronicle (Google): petabyte-scale retention at flat pricing — compelling for environments with extremely high log volumes.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 2;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'XDR and EDR unify detection across endpoint, network, email, and cloud. Learn how behavioral analysis, process trees, and threat hunting combine to find adversaries before they strike.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'XDR & EDR — Endpoint Telemetry, Behavior Analysis & Threat Hunting',
      'description', 'XDR and EDR unify detection across endpoint, network, email, and cloud. Learn how behavioral analysis, process trees, and threat hunting combine to find adversaries before them before they strike.',
      'transcript', 'EDR (Endpoint Detection & Response) was the first major evolution beyond traditional antivirus: instead of signature-based detection (does this file match a known malware hash?), EDR records everything happening on an endpoint — every process created, every file written, every network connection made, every registry key modified — and uses behavioral analysis to detect threats that have never been seen before.

The EDR agent runs on every managed endpoint: laptops, servers, cloud VMs. It sends a continuous stream of telemetry to a central analysis platform. When the analysis engine detects suspicious behavior — a Word document spawning PowerShell, which spawns a network connection to an external IP — it creates an alert and provides the process tree: the full chain of parent and child processes, with timestamps, command lines, file hashes, and network connections. This process tree is the most important forensic artifact in modern incident response. It lets an analyst reconstruct exactly what happened from initial execution to every downstream action in seconds, rather than hours of log correlation.

Behavioral detection uses machine learning models trained on millions of malicious and benign execution patterns. Instead of "does this hash match a known bad file?", behavioral detection asks "does this sequence of actions match the pattern of a threat actor''s post-exploitation behavior?" This is why EDR catches fileless malware (malicious code that runs entirely in memory, never writing to disk), living-off-the-land attacks (using legitimate Windows tools like PowerShell, WMI, and certutil for malicious purposes), and zero-day exploits (novel vulnerabilities with no existing signatures).

EDR platforms differ significantly. CrowdStrike Falcon is the market leader in detection efficacy: its Threat Graph correlates telemetry across all Falcon-enrolled endpoints globally, providing a network effect where a technique detected on one customer''s endpoint creates a detection for all customers within minutes. Microsoft Defender for Endpoint integrates natively with M365 and Entra ID, making it the default choice for Microsoft-centric organizations. SentinelOne Singularity has the strongest autonomous response capabilities — it can automatically quarantine a compromised process, kill a malicious thread, and roll back ransomware-encrypted files using its rollback feature.

XDR (Extended Detection & Response) extends EDR by unifying telemetry from endpoints, network, email, identity, and cloud into a single investigation surface. The value of XDR is correlation across domains that previously required manual analyst effort to connect: an email phishing alert + an endpoint execution alert + an identity alert (impossible travel) + a cloud storage access alert — in a legacy environment, these four alerts arrive in four different consoles and require a Tier 3 analyst to manually correlate. XDR correlates them automatically into a single incident with a unified attack story.

Threat hunting is proactive rather than reactive: instead of waiting for an alert to fire, a threat hunter forms a hypothesis based on threat intelligence or a known attacker TTP, then searches the telemetry for evidence that the activity has occurred. Example hunt: "We know APT29 uses scheduled tasks for persistence. Let me search all endpoint telemetry for scheduled tasks created in the last 30 days that run PowerShell or cmd.exe, were created by non-administrative accounts, and have not been inventoried in our asset management system." This search finds attacker persistence that signature-based detection and rules-based alerting missed entirely.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 3;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Threat intelligence transforms raw attack data into actionable knowledge. Master the intelligence lifecycle, STIX/TAXII, IOC vs TTP distinction, and building an enterprise CTI program.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Threat Intelligence — STIX/TAXII, IOCs, TTPs & the Intelligence Lifecycle',
      'description', 'Threat intelligence transforms raw attack data into actionable knowledge. Master the intelligence lifecycle, STIX/TAXII, IOC vs TTP distinction, and building an enterprise CTI program.',
      'transcript', 'Threat intelligence is finished information about adversaries — who they are, what they want, how they operate, and what indicators they leave behind. The distinction between data and intelligence is critical: a list of malicious IP addresses is data. An analysis of which threat actor group uses those IPs, what their targeting criteria are, what their techniques are after initial access, and which of your assets they would most likely target — that is intelligence. Intelligence is actionable; data alone is noise.

The intelligence lifecycle has six stages. Direction: define what intelligence you need. What threat actors are most likely to target your industry? What attack vectors do they use? What are your highest-value assets that adversaries would want? These questions define your intelligence requirements — the questions your CTI program must answer. Collection: gather raw data from sources — open source (OSINT), dark web monitoring, vendor threat feeds, ISACs (sector-specific information sharing groups), honeypots, and incident response findings. Processing: normalize, deduplicate, and enrich raw data — convert IP addresses to ASN and geolocation, resolve domains to registration history, hash files to malware families. Analysis: identify patterns, attribute activity to threat actor groups, assess likelihood and impact. Production: create finished intelligence products — strategic (what threat landscape does the CEO need to understand?), operational (what specific campaigns are targeting our sector this month?), tactical (which IOCs should be added to our SIEM detection rules today?). Dissemination and feedback complete the cycle.

IOCs (Indicators of Compromise) are the artifacts left by attackers: IP addresses, domain names, file hashes, email sender addresses, and URL patterns. IOCs are high-confidence when fresh but decay rapidly — adversaries change infrastructure frequently. A SIEM rule blocking a known-bad IP is valuable for 48 hours; that same IP will be abandoned by the adversary and recycled by a legitimate service within weeks. IOCs are tactical intelligence — useful for immediate detection and blocking, but they do not tell you how an adversary will attack you next.

TTPs (Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures) are the behavior patterns of adversaries — how they operate rather than the specific tools they use. TTPs are durable: an adversary can change their malware hash and C2 server IP within hours, but changing their fundamental attack methodology takes months of retooling. MITRE ATT&CK is the universal framework for documenting and communicating TTPs — 14 tactics, 193 techniques, 411 sub-techniques. Detecting TTPs rather than IOCs is the difference between reactive security (blocking yesterday''s attack) and proactive security (detecting today''s attack method).

STIX (Structured Threat Information eXpression) is the standard data format for threat intelligence. STIX 2.1 represents threat actors, campaigns, malware, attack patterns, indicators, and relationships as JSON objects linked by relationship objects — creating a knowledge graph of the threat landscape. TAXII (Trusted Automated eXchange of Intelligence Information) is the transport protocol: it defines how STIX objects are shared between systems and organizations in a standardized way, enabling automated machine-to-machine intelligence sharing.

Building an enterprise CTI program: start with threat actor profiling — identify the five most likely threat actors targeting your industry and geography, document their TTPs in ATT&CK Navigator, and use this profile to prioritize your detection engineering backlog. Subscribe to a premium intelligence feed (Recorded Future, Mandiant Advantage, CrowdStrike Falcon Intelligence) for operational intelligence. Join your sector ISAC for peer intelligence sharing. Build automated IOC ingestion pipelines into your SIEM for tactical intelligence. The output: a detection stack tuned to the adversaries most likely to actually attack you.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 4;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Incident response determines whether a breach costs $50K or $50M. Master the NIST SP 800-61 lifecycle, build an IR playbook, and run a tabletop exercise that prepares your team for the real thing.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Incident Response — NIST SP 800-61 & the IR Playbook',
      'description', 'Incident response determines whether a breach costs $50K or $50M. Master the NIST SP 800-61 lifecycle, build an IR playbook, and run a tabletop exercise that prepares your team for the real thing.',
      'transcript', 'Incident response is the organization''s capability to detect, contain, eradicate, and recover from security incidents in a way that minimizes business impact and preserves evidence. The difference between organizations that survive major breaches and those that do not is rarely the sophistication of the attack — it is the effectiveness of the response.

NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2 is the foundational incident response standard. It defines four phases. Phase 1 — Preparation: before an incident occurs, you build the capability. This includes forming an Incident Response Team (IRT), writing and training on IR playbooks, establishing communication protocols, acquiring and maintaining forensic tools, and running tabletop exercises. Preparation is where most organizations underinvest — they discover their IR plan is untested the moment they need it. Phase 2 — Detection & Analysis: identify that an incident has occurred and determine its scope. Is this a confirmed incident or a false positive? What systems are affected? What is the timeline of events? What is the severity and business impact? Phase 3 — Containment, Eradication & Recovery: stop the spread, eliminate the threat, and restore operations. Short-term containment (isolate affected systems), long-term containment (apply patches, reset credentials, harden configurations), eradication (remove malware, close access vectors), and recovery (restore from clean backup, verify integrity, return to production). Phase 4 — Post-Incident Activity: the lessons learned meeting, the formal incident report, and the control improvements that prevent recurrence.

An IR playbook documents the step-by-step response procedures for specific incident types. At minimum, you need playbooks for: ransomware, phishing with credential compromise, data exfiltration, business email compromise (BEC), insider threat, and DDoS. Each playbook specifies: detection signatures (how will we know this incident type has occurred?), initial triage steps (what do we do in the first 30 minutes?), containment actions (which specific systems to isolate, which accounts to disable, which network segments to block?), evidence preservation (what to capture before containment potentially destroys evidence?), escalation criteria (when does the incident escalate to the CISO? when does legal need to be notified?), and communication templates (what do we tell affected customers? what goes to the board?).

Incident declaration criteria prevent both under-reaction and over-reaction. Define threshold conditions for each severity level: P1 (Critical — active ransomware spreading, confirmed data exfiltration, CEO account compromised), P2 (High — confirmed malware on a production server, evidence of unauthorized access to sensitive data), P3 (Medium — phishing email with click but no credential submission, malware on an isolated non-production system), P4 (Low — phishing email blocked, no interaction). Each severity level has defined response timelines and escalation paths.

Tabletop exercises are the most effective way to test your IR capability without a real incident. Gather your IRT and walk through a realistic attack scenario: "An employee reports their laptop is encrypting files. This is a Tuesday morning at 8:47 AM. Your SIEM shows 23 other endpoints with similar behavior. The domain controller is not yet affected. Walk me through your next 60 minutes." Tabletops surface gaps in playbooks, communication failures, tool access issues, and decision-making bottlenecks before they matter. Run them quarterly. The scenarios should evolve to reflect current threat actor TTPs.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 5;

-- ─── MODULE 3: Cloud Security — AWS, Azure, GCP & Container Security ─────────

  SELECT id INTO v_ch FROM public.chapters
    WHERE course_id = v_cid ORDER BY order_index LIMIT 1 OFFSET 2;
  IF v_ch IS NULL THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'M03 chapter not found'; END IF;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'The shared responsibility model defines the boundary between what cloud providers secure and what you must secure. Get this wrong and your cloud migration becomes your biggest security liability.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Cloud Security Fundamentals & Shared Responsibility Model',
      'description', 'The shared responsibility model defines the boundary between what cloud providers secure and what you must secure. Get this wrong and your cloud migration becomes your biggest security liability.',
      'transcript', 'Cloud security begins with understanding a single foundational concept: the shared responsibility model. Every cloud provider — AWS, Azure, GCP — uses this model to define exactly who is responsible for securing what. Getting this wrong is the source of most cloud breaches.

The cloud provider is always responsible for security OF the cloud: the physical data centers, the hardware, the hypervisor, the underlying network infrastructure, and the global networking fabric. You cannot access or control these layers, and the provider takes full responsibility for their security. AWS, Azure, and GCP invest billions annually in physical security, hardware security, and infrastructure security because a compromise at that layer would affect millions of customers simultaneously.

The customer is responsible for security IN the cloud: everything you build and configure on top of the cloud provider''s infrastructure. This includes: your data (classification, encryption, access controls), your identity configurations (IAM policies, roles, permissions — the most breach-prone layer), your operating systems and applications running on VMs, your network configurations (security groups, NACLs, VPC design), and your serverless function code. The scope of customer responsibility narrows as you move from IaaS (Infrastructure as a Service — you manage OS and everything above) to PaaS (Platform as a Service — you manage applications and data only) to SaaS (Software as a Service — you manage user access and data governance only).

Misconfiguration is the dominant cloud threat. Verizon''s Data Breach Investigations Report consistently identifies misconfiguration as the top cause of cloud incidents. Common misconfigurations: public S3 buckets (AWS defaults changed in 2023, but legacy buckets remain public in many environments), overpermissive IAM policies (using AdministratorAccess where ReadOnlyAccess would suffice), open security groups (allowing 0.0.0.0/0 inbound on port 22 or 3389), unencrypted EBS volumes or RDS databases, and CloudTrail logging disabled (blind spot for incident response).

CSPM (Cloud Security Posture Management) tools continuously audit your cloud environment against security best practices and compliance frameworks. Instead of a point-in-time manual audit that grows stale within days, CSPM provides real-time visibility into misconfigurations, with findings mapped to CIS Benchmarks, SOC 2 controls, ISO 27001 Annex A, NIST CSF, and GDPR requirements. Major CSPM platforms: Prisma Cloud (Palo Alto), Wiz (fastest-growing), Microsoft Defender for Cloud (built into Azure, multi-cloud capable), and Lacework.

Cloud security architecture principles: apply Zero Trust at every layer — never trust network location, always verify identity. Use cloud-native security services first (AWS GuardDuty, Azure Defender, GCP Security Command Center) before adding third-party tools — native services have the deepest integration and lowest operational overhead. Design for least privilege from day one — it is far harder to reduce permissions in an existing environment than to start restricted and expand as needed. Enable logging everywhere — CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs, DNS logs, and application logs — because you cannot investigate what you did not log. Test your configurations with automated scanning in CI/CD pipelines so misconfigurations are caught before they reach production.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 1;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'AWS is the world''s largest cloud platform. Master IAM policies, GuardDuty threat detection, Security Hub compliance aggregation, KMS encryption, and CloudTrail audit logging.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'AWS Security — IAM, GuardDuty, Security Hub, KMS & CloudTrail',
      'description', 'AWS is the world''s largest cloud platform. Master IAM policies, GuardDuty threat detection, Security Hub compliance aggregation, KMS encryption, and CloudTrail audit logging.',
      'transcript', 'AWS holds approximately 31% of the global cloud market. Securing AWS environments is a core skill for any enterprise security professional, and AWS provides a comprehensive native security stack that, when properly configured, provides enterprise-grade protection without requiring third-party tools.

AWS IAM (Identity and Access Management) is the most critical AWS security component and the most common source of breaches. IAM controls who (identity) can do what (action) on which (resource) under what conditions (condition). IAM policies are JSON documents that explicitly allow or deny specific API actions on specific resources. The key principles for secure IAM: never use the root account for day-to-day operations (create an IAM user or use SSO instead), enforce MFA on all human users, use IAM roles (temporary credentials) rather than IAM users (long-lived credentials) for applications and services, apply AWS Organizations Service Control Policies (SCPs) to set permission guardrails across all accounts, and use IAM Access Analyzer to identify resources exposed to external accounts. Least privilege in practice: start with ReadOnlyAccess and add specific permissions as needed, never start with AdministratorAccess and remove later.

AWS GuardDuty is a managed threat detection service that analyzes CloudTrail events, VPC Flow Logs, and DNS query logs to identify malicious activity. GuardDuty uses machine learning, anomaly detection, and integrated threat intelligence to surface findings like: cryptocurrency mining (EC2 instance calling a mining pool IP), credential exfiltration (API calls from an unusual geographic location immediately after CloudTrail logging was disabled), and reconnaissance (port scanning from an EC2 instance to the internet). GuardDuty is enabled per-region and per-account — Organizations integration allows enabling it centrally across all accounts. Cost is based on data volume analyzed, not a flat fee.

AWS Security Hub aggregates security findings from GuardDuty, Amazon Inspector (vulnerability scanning), Amazon Macie (S3 sensitive data discovery), AWS Config (configuration compliance), and third-party CSPM tools into a single dashboard. Security Hub evaluates your environment against security standards: CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark, AWS Foundational Security Best Practices, and PCI DSS. Each finding includes a severity score, a remediation recommendation, and an AWS Config rule that would prevent the issue. Security Hub is the single pane of glass for AWS security posture.

AWS KMS (Key Management Service) manages encryption keys for AWS services. Every AWS service that encrypts data at rest (S3, EBS, RDS, DynamoDB, Secrets Manager, CloudTrail) can use KMS. KMS provides: centralized key management, automatic annual key rotation, detailed key usage logging in CloudTrail, and access control via key policies (who can use or manage each key). Customer Managed Keys (CMKs) give you control over key policy and lifecycle. AWS Managed Keys are created and managed automatically by AWS services but are less configurable. CloudHSM provides dedicated hardware security modules for the highest-assurance key management requirements.

CloudTrail is the audit log for every AWS API call made in your account. Without CloudTrail, you have no way to answer "who changed this security group?", "who deleted this S3 bucket?", or "where did these access keys originate?" CloudTrail events record: who made the API call (user identity), when (timestamp), what (API action and parameters), from where (source IP), and whether it succeeded (response code). Enable CloudTrail in all regions (not just your primary region), enable log file validation (to detect tampering), send logs to a dedicated centralized logging account (so an attacker who compromises an application account cannot delete CloudTrail logs), and set an S3 lifecycle policy to retain logs for the compliance period (typically 1–7 years).'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 2;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Azure''s native security stack protects hybrid and cloud-native environments. Master Defender for Cloud, Entra ID Conditional Access, Microsoft Sentinel, and Azure Policy.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Azure Security — Defender for Cloud, Entra ID, Sentinel & Policies',
      'description', 'Azure''s native security stack protects hybrid and cloud-native environments. Master Defender for Cloud, Entra ID Conditional Access, Microsoft Sentinel, and Azure Policy.',
      'transcript', 'Microsoft Azure is the second-largest cloud platform globally and the dominant platform in enterprise environments due to its Microsoft 365 integration, Active Directory compatibility, and strong hybrid cloud capabilities. Azure''s native security stack is one of the most comprehensive available — Microsoft spends over $1B annually on cybersecurity research and tooling.

Microsoft Entra ID (formerly Azure Active Directory) is the identity fabric of the Microsoft cloud ecosystem. Every Azure subscription, Microsoft 365 tenant, and Entra-integrated application uses Entra ID for authentication and authorization. Entra ID''s security capabilities: Conditional Access (risk-based MFA enforcement — require MFA when signing in from an unknown device, anomalous location, or after a risky sign-in detected by Microsoft''s threat intelligence), Identity Protection (automatically detects and remediates compromised accounts — leaked credentials, anonymous proxy access, impossible travel), Privileged Identity Management (PIM — just-in-time elevation for Azure admin roles, requiring approval and MFA for privileged access), and External Identities (B2B collaboration with guest users from external organizations).

Microsoft Defender for Cloud (formerly Azure Security Center + Azure Defender) provides CSPM and CWPP (Cloud Workload Protection Platform) in one service. CSPM component: continuous assessment of Azure resources against security benchmarks (Microsoft Cloud Security Benchmark, CIS, NIST CSF, PCI DSS, SOC 2), with a Secure Score that gives your environment a 0–100 security rating and prioritized remediation recommendations. CWPP component: workload protection for VMs (Defender for Servers), SQL databases (Defender for SQL), storage accounts (Defender for Storage), containers (Defender for Containers), Key Vault, DNS, and App Service. Multi-cloud: Defender for Cloud now extends to AWS and GCP environments, making it the unified CSPM for hybrid and multi-cloud organizations.

Microsoft Sentinel is Azure''s cloud-native SIEM/SOAR. Deep integration advantages: native ingestion from M365 (Exchange Online, Teams, SharePoint), Entra ID sign-in and audit logs, Defender for Endpoint, Defender for Cloud, and Azure Activity Log — all without parser configuration. Sentinel uses KUSTO Query Language (KQL) for log analysis — powerful, readable, and optimized for large-scale log data. Sentinel Workbooks provide pre-built security dashboards. Sentinel Analytics rules trigger alerts. Sentinel Playbooks (built on Azure Logic Apps) automate response actions — when an alert fires, a Playbook can automatically disable the user in Entra ID, block the IP in the firewall, and create a Jira ticket within seconds of detection.

Azure Policy enforces organizational standards on Azure resources. Policies define rules: "Storage accounts must use HTTPS only", "VMs must have disk encryption enabled", "Public IP addresses cannot be assigned to production NICs". Policy effect options range from Audit (flag violations without blocking) to Deny (prevent non-compliant resources from being created or modified) to DeployIfNotExists (automatically deploy required configuration on compliant resources). Azure Policy Initiatives bundle multiple policies into a single assignment — the Azure Security Benchmark initiative contains 200+ policies enforcing security best practices across all resource types. Policy assignments can be scoped to Management Groups, Subscriptions, or Resource Groups, and evaluated results feed into Defender for Cloud''s compliance dashboard.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 3;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Containers are everywhere and vulnerable by default. Master Docker hardening, image scanning with Trivy, Kubernetes RBAC, network policies, and pod security admission.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Container Security — Docker Hardening, Image Scanning & Kubernetes RBAC',
      'description', 'Containers are everywhere and vulnerable by default. Master Docker hardening, image scanning with Trivy, Kubernetes RBAC, network policies, and pod security admission.',
      'transcript', 'Containers have fundamentally changed application deployment — and fundamentally changed the attack surface. The ability to package an application and its dependencies into a portable image that runs consistently anywhere is enormously powerful. But containers introduce unique security risks that traditional VM security models do not address, and most container environments are deployed with security as an afterthought.

Container attack surface: image vulnerabilities (outdated base images with known CVEs), embedded secrets (API keys, database passwords hardcoded in Dockerfiles or environment variables), root containers (processes running as root inside the container inherit host root privileges if the container is compromised), excessive capabilities (Linux capabilities granted that the application does not need), and runtime threats (malicious code injecting into a running container or exploiting container runtime vulnerabilities to escape to the host).

Docker hardening starts at the image level. Use minimal base images — distroless images (Google''s distroless project) contain only the application binary and its runtime dependencies, with no shell, no package manager, and no OS utilities for an attacker to use. Multi-stage builds separate the build environment (which needs compilers, test tools, dev dependencies) from the production image (which needs only the compiled binary). Specify a non-root USER in the Dockerfile — running as UID 1000 rather than root means that even if the container is compromised, the attacker does not have root-level filesystem access. Set the filesystem to read-only (--read-only flag) unless the application requires write access to specific directories. Remove all debugging tools and package managers from production images.

Image scanning with Trivy (open-source, fast, comprehensive) or Grype analyzes container images for known CVEs in OS packages and language libraries before deployment. Integrate scanning in CI/CD: build the image, scan it, fail the pipeline if critical CVEs are present (no CVSS 9.0+ in production), push to registry only if scan passes. Container registries with built-in scanning: ECR (AWS), ACR (Azure), Artifact Registry (GCP), Docker Hub. Runtime scanning: Falco (open-source, CNCF project) monitors container runtime behavior and alerts on anomalous activity — a container executing a shell, reading /etc/shadow, or making an unexpected network connection.

Kubernetes RBAC controls which users and service accounts can perform which actions on which Kubernetes resources. Subjects (users, groups, service accounts) are bound to Roles (namespace-scoped) or ClusterRoles (cluster-scoped) via RoleBindings or ClusterRoleBindings. Least privilege in Kubernetes: each pod should run with a dedicated service account that has only the Kubernetes API permissions it needs — typically none, for most application pods. Default service accounts have no permissions in well-configured clusters. Common RBAC mistake: binding all service accounts to cluster-admin because it''s easy, giving any compromised pod full cluster control.

Kubernetes network policies control traffic flow between pods. By default, all pods in a Kubernetes cluster can communicate with all other pods — flat networking that allows unlimited lateral movement. Network policies implement micro-segmentation: define which pods can communicate with which other pods, on which ports, in which direction. Pod security admission (PodSecurity admission plugin in Kubernetes 1.25+) enforces security contexts at admission time: Privileged (no restrictions), Baseline (prevents known privilege escalations), and Restricted (heavily restricted, most secure, follows current hardening best practices). Set namespaces to Restricted unless the workload explicitly requires exceptions.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 4;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'CSPM provides continuous cloud security visibility. Master cloud security posture management tools, secrets management with HashiCorp Vault, and building a cloud-native security architecture.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Cloud CSPM, Secrets Management & Cloud-Native Security Posture',
      'description', 'CSPM provides continuous cloud security visibility. Master cloud security posture management tools, secrets management with HashiCorp Vault, and building a cloud-native security architecture.',
      'transcript', 'Cloud Security Posture Management has become an essential capability for any organization running workloads in cloud environments. The scale and velocity of cloud infrastructure — new resources provisioned in seconds, configurations changed by automation pipelines, permissions modified by multiple teams simultaneously — makes manual security audits inadequate. CSPM provides continuous, automated visibility into the security posture of your cloud environment.

CSPM tools work by connecting to your cloud accounts via APIs (not agents) and continuously querying the configuration of every resource: S3 bucket public access settings, security group rules, IAM policy assignments, encryption configuration on databases, logging settings on cloud services, network access control lists, and hundreds of other configuration points. Each configuration is evaluated against a library of security checks mapped to CIS Benchmarks, NIST SP 800-53, SOC 2 TSC, ISO 27001 Annex A, PCI DSS, and GDPR. Findings are prioritized by severity and business context: a misconfiguration on an internet-facing production database handling PII is a Critical finding; the same misconfiguration on a development environment with no real data is a Low.

Wiz has become the fastest-growing CSPM vendor because of its "security graph" approach. Instead of generating a list of individual misconfiguration findings, Wiz maps the entire cloud environment as a graph — every resource, every connection, every permission relationship — and identifies attack paths: combinations of findings that, together, create an exploitable path to a sensitive asset. A single public S3 bucket is a finding; the same S3 bucket exposed to a Lambda function that has an IAM role with EC2 admin rights that can reach the production database — that is an attack path that would allow an unauthenticated attacker to reach your production database via four misconfigured components, none of which is Critical in isolation.

Secrets management is one of the highest-impact security controls available. Secrets are credentials, API keys, database passwords, certificates, and tokens that applications use to authenticate to other systems. The default behavior — storing secrets in environment variables, configuration files, or (catastrophically) source code — creates a massive breach surface: a code repository breach, a misconfigured application endpoint, or an insider threat can expose every system credential simultaneously. Secrets management platforms (HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault, GCP Secret Manager) provide: centralized storage with encryption at rest, fine-grained access control (which application can retrieve which secret?), automatic rotation (database passwords rotated on a schedule without application downtime), dynamic secrets (Vault generates short-lived credentials on-demand — the application gets a database password that expires in 15 minutes), and a full audit trail of every secret access event.

HashiCorp Vault architecture: the Vault server stores and manages secrets. Applications authenticate to Vault using an auth method (AWS IAM role, Kubernetes service account, AppRole, or LDAP) and receive a short-lived token. That token is used to retrieve the specific secrets the application is authorized to access. Vault then records the authentication event, the token issuance, and the secret access in its audit log. Dynamic secrets for databases: Vault connects to your database, creates a new user account with a time-limited password, and provides those credentials to the requesting application. When the lease expires, Vault revokes the credentials automatically — no static database passwords, no credential accumulation.

Cloud-native security architecture principles: treat infrastructure as code (Terraform, Pulumi, CloudFormation) and apply security scanning to IaC before deployment (Checkov, tfsec, Terrascan). Never provision cloud resources manually — every manual change is an undocumented change and a security drift risk. Use immutable infrastructure: instead of patching running instances, replace them with freshly provisioned instances from a hardened golden image. Build security monitoring into the deployment pipeline: every deployment triggers a CSPM scan of the affected resources. Cloud security is a continuous process, not a one-time audit.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 5;

-- ─── MODULE 4: Network Security & Penetration Testing ────────────────────────

  SELECT id INTO v_ch FROM public.chapters
    WHERE course_id = v_cid ORDER BY order_index LIMIT 1 OFFSET 3;
  IF v_ch IS NULL THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'M04 chapter not found'; END IF;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Network security is the bedrock of enterprise defense. Master the TCP/IP attack surface, network segmentation architecture, IDS/IPS deployment, and designing a zero-trust network.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Network Security Fundamentals — Protocols, Attack Vectors & Defense Architecture',
      'description', 'Network security is the bedrock of enterprise defense. Master the TCP/IP attack surface, network segmentation architecture, IDS/IPS deployment, and designing a zero-trust network.',
      'transcript', 'Network security is the discipline of protecting data in transit and controlling traffic flows across your infrastructure. While the shift to cloud and Zero Trust architecture has reduced reliance on network perimeters as the primary security boundary, network security remains essential: attackers who compromise a host inside your network use network protocols for lateral movement, data exfiltration, and command-and-control communication. Detecting and blocking those activities is a network security problem.

The TCP/IP protocol stack was designed for functionality and reliability, not security. Every layer has exploitable weaknesses. At the link layer: ARP spoofing — an attacker sends gratuitous ARP replies poisoning the ARP cache of hosts on the local subnet, redirecting traffic through the attacker''s machine for man-in-the-middle interception. VLAN hopping: in environments with default switch configurations, an attacker can trunk a switch port to gain access to VLANs beyond their own. At the network layer: ICMP redirect attacks, IP spoofing, and BGP hijacking (redirecting global internet routing). At the transport layer: TCP SYN flooding (exhausting server connection tables), TCP session hijacking. At the application layer: DNS poisoning, HTTP header injection, SSL stripping.

Network segmentation is the foundational control that limits the blast radius of any breach. A flat network — where all hosts can communicate with all other hosts — allows a single compromised endpoint to reach every other host. A properly segmented network divides the environment into security zones: the DMZ (internet-facing services, separated from the internal network), the internal production network (further segmented by application tier — web tier, application tier, database tier — with firewall rules permitting only necessary inter-tier traffic), the internal corporate network (user workstations, separated from production), the OT/IoT network (operational technology devices, isolated from IT systems), and the management network (administrative access to all other segments, most restricted).

Firewall architecture implements segmentation. Stateful inspection firewalls track the state of network connections and only allow traffic that belongs to established, authorized sessions. Next-generation firewalls (NGFWs) add application awareness (identify applications regardless of port), user identification (map traffic to specific users), intrusion prevention, and SSL inspection (decrypt and inspect HTTPS traffic). Firewall rule management: maintain least-privilege rules, audit rules quarterly for rules that reference decommissioned systems or services, and log all denied traffic for SIEM analysis.

IDS (Intrusion Detection System) monitors network traffic passively and generates alerts when suspicious patterns are detected. IPS (Intrusion Prevention System) actively blocks suspicious traffic in-line. Signature-based detection: match traffic patterns against a database of known attack signatures (Snort, Suricata rule sets). Anomaly-based detection: baseline normal traffic patterns and alert on deviations. Network TAPs or SPAN ports provide the IDS/IPS with a copy of traffic to inspect. Placement: at perimeter (north-south traffic between internet and internal network) and at segment boundaries (east-west traffic between internal network zones).

Zero Trust network architecture removes implicit network trust entirely. ZTNA (Zero Trust Network Access) replaces VPN: instead of granting network-level access to the internal network (which then allows lateral movement to any resource), ZTNA grants access to specific applications only, after identity verification and device health check, regardless of network location. Software-Defined Perimeter (SDP) implements ZTNA: every access request is authenticated and authorized before a connection is established, and each connection is point-to-point between the verified user and the specific application. No network access to other resources. No lateral movement possible. The attack surface is reduced from the entire internal network to the specific application the user is authorized to access.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 1;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Penetration testing is authorized adversary simulation. Master the PTES framework, scoping best practices, rules of engagement, and the professional methodology that separates real pen testers from script kiddies.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Penetration Testing Methodology — PTES, Scoping & Rules of Engagement',
      'description', 'Penetration testing is authorized adversary simulation. Master the PTES framework, scoping best practices, rules of engagement, and the professional methodology that separates real pen testers from script kiddies.',
      'transcript', 'Penetration testing is the authorized simulation of a real attacker''s techniques to identify and demonstrate exploitable vulnerabilities before a real adversary finds them. The key word is authorized — every action in a penetration test must be explicitly permitted by the organization''s legal owner of the systems being tested. A penetration test without written authorization is a crime regardless of intent.

PTES (Penetration Testing Execution Standard) is the professional methodology framework that structures a penetration test from initial engagement to final report. Seven phases: Pre-engagement (scope definition, legal authorization, team assembly, logistics), Intelligence Gathering (passive and active reconnaissance), Threat Modeling (identify likely attack paths based on gathered intelligence), Vulnerability Analysis (systematic identification of exploitable weaknesses), Exploitation (active exploitation of identified vulnerabilities), Post-Exploitation (demonstrating business impact of successful exploitation), and Reporting (documenting findings in a way that drives remediation).

The scoping process defines precisely what will and will not be tested. Scope documents must specify: IP ranges and hostnames in scope, explicitly excluded systems (production databases, healthcare systems, payment processors that must not be disrupted), testing windows (business hours only? 24/7? avoid month-end close?), geographic restrictions (only test from specific IP addresses or locations), and specific techniques that are and are not permitted (social engineering permitted? physical security testing? denial-of-service testing excluded?). Vague scope is a liability for everyone involved — the tester can accidentally cause business disruption, and the organization cannot hold anyone accountable for out-of-scope damage.

Rules of Engagement (RoE) document the behavioral constraints of the engagement. Key RoE elements: escalation path (if a critical system is compromised, who does the pen tester notify immediately?), emergency contacts (who to call if the test causes unintended disruption?), evidence handling (how will screenshots, captured credentials, and sensitive data be handled and destroyed after the engagement?), third-party notifications (does the test scope include cloud infrastructure that requires notifying the cloud provider under their terms of service?), and pivot limitations (if a compromised host provides access to out-of-scope systems, stop and notify the client rather than continuing).

Types of penetration tests by knowledge: Black Box (tester has no prior knowledge — simulates an external attacker with no insider information), Grey Box (tester has some knowledge, such as a network diagram or one set of low-privilege credentials — most common engagement type), White Box (tester has full access to network diagrams, architecture documentation, and source code — most thorough, used for secure SDLC testing). Types by scope: External (testing internet-facing systems from outside the network), Internal (testing from inside the network — simulates a compromised insider or post-breach lateral movement scenario), Web Application (focused on a specific application — OWASP methodology), Social Engineering (phishing campaigns, pretexting calls, physical access attempts).

The pen test kick-off meeting aligns expectations before testing begins. Agenda: confirm scope and RoE signatures, exchange emergency contact information, confirm testing window start time, establish check-in protocol (daily status reports or only on critical findings?), clarify what constitutes a "critical finding requiring immediate notification" (e.g., domain administrator access achieved, active ransomware indicator found), and confirm testing evidence delivery method (encrypted channel only). Document everything — scope creep requests during testing must be written and signed, not agreed verbally.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 2;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Reconnaissance is where penetration tests are won or lost. Master passive OSINT, active scanning with Nmap and Nessus, CVSS scoring, and building an intelligence-driven vulnerability prioritization model.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Reconnaissance & Vulnerability Assessment — Active & Passive Intelligence',
      'description', 'Reconnaissance is where penetration tests are won or lost. Master passive OSINT, active scanning with Nmap and Nessus, CVSS scoring, and building an intelligence-driven vulnerability prioritization model.',
      'transcript', 'Reconnaissance is the first active phase of a penetration test and the foundation on which every subsequent phase depends. The quality of your reconnaissance determines which attack paths you identify, which vulnerabilities you prioritize, and ultimately whether you achieve your objectives. Professional pen testers invest heavily in recon — it is not a quick step before the "real work" of exploitation.

Passive reconnaissance gathers intelligence without sending any packets to the target. Sources: Shodan (search engine for internet-connected devices — reveals open ports, running services, operating system versions, TLS certificate details, and geographic location without touching the target network), Censys (similar to Shodan, with better certificate intelligence), WHOIS (domain registration information — registrant name, email, address, nameservers, registration dates), certificate transparency logs (every TLS certificate ever issued is logged publicly — search crt.sh for all certificates issued for a target domain, revealing subdomains not in public DNS), LinkedIn (employee names, job titles, technologies they list — critical for social engineering target selection and understanding the technology stack), and Google dorking (advanced Google search operators to find exposed files, login pages, and sensitive information indexed by Google).

OSINT (Open Source Intelligence) for employee enumeration: LinkedIn searches for target company employees reveal technology experience (an employee listing "Palo Alto Firewalls" and "CrowdStrike" tells you about the security stack), reveal names for phishing pretexts, and identify the organizational structure. theHarvester is a command-line tool that aggregates emails, subdomains, and employee names from search engines, LinkedIn, PGP key servers, and other sources. Breached credential databases (HaveIBeenPwned API, DeHashed) check whether target organization emails appear in known breach datasets — compromised credentials are the most common initial access vector.

Active reconnaissance sends packets to the target. Nmap is the standard port scanner: nmap -sV -sC -O -p- scans all 65,535 TCP ports, detects service versions (-sV), runs default NSE scripts (-sC), and attempts OS detection (-O). Service enumeration: once ports are identified, deeper probing determines exact service versions, which maps to CVE databases. SMB enumeration (nmap SMB scripts, enum4linux) reveals Windows domain information, share listings, user accounts. SNMP scanning (v1/v2c with public community strings) can expose network device configurations. Web application fingerprinting (WhatWeb, Wappalyzer) identifies web frameworks, CMS versions, and JavaScript libraries.

Vulnerability assessment tools (Nessus, Qualys, OpenVAS) combine port scanning with a database of known vulnerabilities matched to detected service versions. Nessus Professional runs authenticated scans (providing OS-level credentials to the scanner, allowing it to read installed software versions, registry settings, and patch levels) and unauthenticated scans (external attacker perspective — only what is visible without credentials). Authenticated scans find significantly more vulnerabilities — 60–80% of vulnerabilities are only visible from an authenticated perspective.

CVSS (Common Vulnerability Scoring System) v3.1 provides a 0–10 severity score based on: Base Metrics (exploitability — attack vector, attack complexity, privileges required, user interaction; and impact — confidentiality, integrity, availability), Temporal Metrics (exploit code maturity, remediation level, report confidence), and Environmental Metrics (adjusted for your specific environment). Critical = 9.0–10.0, High = 7.0–8.9, Medium = 4.0–6.9, Low = 0.1–3.9. CVSS alone is insufficient for prioritization — a CVSS 9.8 vulnerability on an isolated development system with no internet access is far less urgent than a CVSS 7.5 vulnerability on an internet-facing production system with known public exploits and access to a PII database.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 3;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Post-exploitation demonstrates real business impact. Learn Metasploit, privilege escalation techniques, credential harvesting, lateral movement, and how to collect irrefutable evidence for your report.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Exploitation, Post-Exploitation & Lateral Movement Techniques',
      'description', 'Post-exploitation demonstrates real business impact. Learn Metasploit, privilege escalation techniques, credential harvesting, lateral movement, and how to collect irrefutable evidence for your report.',
      'transcript', 'Exploitation is the phase where identified vulnerabilities are actively leveraged to gain access to target systems. In a professional penetration test, the goal of exploitation is not to cause damage — it is to demonstrate to the client that a vulnerability is genuinely exploitable, not just theoretically present, and to establish a foothold for post-exploitation activities that demonstrate business impact.

Metasploit Framework is the most widely used exploitation platform. It provides: a searchable database of exploit modules (searchsploit and msf search — find modules by CVE, service name, or platform), automated exploit delivery with configurable payloads (Meterpreter — a memory-resident agent that provides a rich post-exploitation interface), auxiliary modules for scanning and enumeration, and post-exploitation modules for privilege escalation, persistence, and credential dumping. Metasploit use in professional testing: select the most reliable exploit module for the identified vulnerability, configure the payload to minimize operational impact (avoid crashing services), test in an isolated environment first if possible, and document every action with timestamps.

Manual exploitation is essential when automated tools fail or when the vulnerability is too recent or too custom to have a Metasploit module. Custom exploit development requires understanding of the underlying vulnerability class: buffer overflows require crafting shellcode for the specific architecture and operating system; SQL injection requires understanding the database backend and constructing payloads that bypass WAF rules; deserialization vulnerabilities require generating gadget chains specific to the Java or .NET runtime version.

Post-exploitation begins after initial access is established. The first goal is situational awareness: what host am I on? (hostname, OS version, patch level), what account do I have? (whoami, id, net user), what network does this host see? (ipconfig/ifconfig, route print, netstat -an), what other hosts are reachable? (arp -a, ping sweep, nmap from the compromised host). This information drives the next phase.

Privilege escalation elevates access from a low-privilege user account to a high-privilege account. Local privilege escalation: exploit a local vulnerability (unquoted service path, insecure file permissions on a service binary, token impersonation via SeImpersonatePrivilege, kernel exploits matched to OS version). Domain privilege escalation: Kerberoasting (request TGS tickets for service accounts with SPNs, then crack offline with Hashcat — service accounts often have weak passwords and high privileges), AS-REP Roasting (exploit accounts with Kerberos pre-authentication disabled), and DCSync (if Domain Replication rights are available, dump all domain password hashes).

Credential harvesting and lateral movement: Mimikatz and similar tools extract credentials from Windows LSASS memory — NTLM hashes, Kerberos tickets, and cleartext passwords (in configurations with WDigest authentication enabled). Pass-the-Hash: use a captured NTLM hash to authenticate without knowing the cleartext password (PsExec, CrackMapExec). Pass-the-Ticket: use a captured Kerberos TGT or TGS to authenticate to services. Lateral movement techniques: PsExec (uses Windows Service Manager), WMI (Windows Management Instrumentation — remote process execution), WinRM (PowerShell remoting), and RDP (Remote Desktop Protocol — if enabled). Evidence collection for the report: timestamped screenshots of every significant access, captured command output saved to a log, and a documented timeline that the client can use to trace the attack path.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 4;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'A penetration test is only as valuable as its report. Learn to write executive summaries, document technical findings with CVSS severity, build remediation roadmaps, and deliver a report that drives real security improvements.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Pen Test Reporting — Executive Summary, Technical Findings & Remediation Roadmap',
      'description', 'A penetration test is only as valuable as its report. Learn to write executive summaries, document technical findings with CVSS severity, build remediation roadmaps, and deliver a report that drives real security improvements.',
      'transcript', 'The penetration test report is the deliverable that determines the value of the engagement. A technically brilliant attack that surfaces critical vulnerabilities is worthless if the report fails to communicate the findings clearly enough to drive remediation. Report writing is a professional skill that separates good pen testers from great ones.

A professional pen test report has two primary audiences with very different needs. Executives (CISOs, CEOs, board members) need to understand business risk: what is the organization''s overall security posture? What are the most critical risks in business terms — financial exposure, regulatory liability, reputational impact? What investment is required to remediate? What is the risk of doing nothing? They should be able to read the report in 15 minutes and understand the organization''s security position without technical knowledge. Technical teams (security engineers, developers, system administrators) need enough detail to reproduce findings and implement fixes: exact vulnerability details, proof-of-concept evidence, specific remediation steps with configuration examples, and enough context to understand why the vulnerability exists and how to prevent recurrence.

Report structure: Executive Summary (1–2 pages, business language, risk-rated summary, top 3 findings, overall risk rating, strategic recommendation), Scope and Methodology (what was tested, what was not tested, testing approach, timeline), Attack Narrative (a chronological story of the test — how did the tester move from initial access to the final objective? This narrative is the most engaging part of the report for technical readers), Findings (each finding on its own page, structured consistently), Appendices (raw scan output, full exploit code, tool versions, tester credentials used).

Each finding should include: Finding Title (clear, descriptive, not technical jargon — "Unauthenticated Remote Code Execution via Apache Log4j" not "CVE-2021-44228"), Risk Rating (Critical/High/Medium/Low/Informational — include CVSS base score and your contextual rating adjusted for the environment), Finding Description (what is the vulnerability? where does it exist? why does it matter?), Proof of Concept (screenshot evidence showing exploitation — what access was achieved, what data was accessible?), Business Impact (what could an attacker do with this access? data exfiltration? ransomware? financial fraud?), Remediation Recommendation (specific, actionable steps — patch version, configuration change, architectural fix), and References (CVE number, vendor advisory, OWASP article).

The remediation roadmap translates findings into a prioritized action plan. Organize findings into three tiers: Immediate (Critical and High findings that require remediation within 30 days — these represent genuine, exploitable risk to critical assets), Short-term (Medium findings, remediate within 90 days — significant risk but harder to exploit or limited impact), Long-term (Low and Informational findings, remediate in the next security program cycle — worth addressing but do not require emergency response). Estimate remediation effort for each finding (hours of engineering work) and present the roadmap as a risk-reduction investment: "Remediating the five Critical findings requires approximately 40 engineering hours and reduces your organizational risk exposure by an estimated 70%."

Retest verification: after the client remediates findings, a retest verifies that the fixes are effective. Document which findings have been remediated (close with evidence), which have been partially remediated (note remaining risk), and which remain open. The retest report becomes the artifact the organization uses for compliance purposes — demonstrating to auditors that identified vulnerabilities were found and fixed.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 5;

-- ─── MODULE 5: Application Security & DevSecOps ──────────────────────────────

  SELECT id INTO v_ch FROM public.chapters
    WHERE course_id = v_cid ORDER BY order_index LIMIT 1 OFFSET 4;
  IF v_ch IS NULL THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'M05 chapter not found'; END IF;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'OWASP Top 10 is the definitive reference for web application security risks. Master all ten categories with real exploitation examples and the specific mitigations that eliminate each vulnerability class.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Application Security Fundamentals & OWASP Top 10 Web',
      'description', 'OWASP Top 10 is the definitive reference for web application security risks. Master all ten categories with real exploitation examples and the specific mitigations that eliminate each vulnerability class.',
      'transcript', 'Application security vulnerabilities are responsible for the vast majority of data breaches. Unlike infrastructure attacks that exploit configuration weaknesses, application-layer attacks exploit flaws in the code that developers wrote — and they work regardless of how well-secured the underlying infrastructure is. OWASP (Open Web Application Security Project) publishes the authoritative Top 10 list of the most critical web application security risks, updated regularly based on analysis of real-world breach data.

A01: Broken Access Control — now the most prevalent web application vulnerability. Occurs when an application fails to properly enforce what authenticated users are allowed to do. Examples: a user can view another user''s account by changing the URL from /account/1234 to /account/1235 (Insecure Direct Object Reference), a normal user can access admin functions by directly navigating to /admin/users (missing function-level authorization), a user can escalate privileges by modifying a hidden form field or JWT claim. Mitigation: implement authorization checks server-side for every request, never trust client-supplied role or permission claims, use indirect object references mapped server-side.

A02: Cryptographic Failures — previously called "Sensitive Data Exposure", renamed to focus on root cause. Weak or absent cryptography exposes sensitive data. Examples: storing passwords with MD5 or SHA-1 (brute-forceable), transmitting sensitive data over HTTP (not HTTPS), using weak encryption modes (ECB mode for block ciphers is deterministic and reveals patterns), storing encryption keys alongside encrypted data. Mitigation: use AES-256 for symmetric encryption, bcrypt/Argon2 for password hashing, enforce TLS 1.2+ (reject older protocol versions), never store sensitive data you do not need.

A03: Injection — SQL injection, command injection, XSS, LDAP injection, XML injection. SQL injection: unsanitized user input incorporated directly into SQL queries allows attackers to manipulate query logic, extract entire databases, or execute operating system commands. Example: login form where username = '' OR 1=1 -- bypasses authentication. Mitigation: parameterized queries (prepared statements) that separate SQL code from user data. Cross-Site Scripting (XSS): unsanitized user input rendered in HTML allows attackers to inject JavaScript executed in victims'' browsers. Mitigation: output encoding, Content Security Policy headers.

A04: Insecure Design — security flaws that exist in the architecture, not just the implementation. No amount of secure coding can fix an insecure design. Example: a password reset flow that relies on a 4-digit PIN sent to an email address can be brute-forced in 10,000 attempts — this is a design flaw, not a code bug. Mitigation: threat modeling during design phase, security design reviews, SDLC security gates before development begins.

A05: Security Misconfiguration — the broadest category: default credentials left unchanged, unnecessary services enabled, verbose error messages exposing stack traces and version information, directory listing enabled on web servers, cloud storage buckets publicly accessible. Mitigation: security configuration baselines, automated CSPM scanning, remove unused features and services.

A06: Vulnerable and Outdated Components — libraries, frameworks, and other components with known vulnerabilities. The Log4Shell vulnerability (CVE-2021-44228, CVSS 10.0) affected virtually every Java application that used the Log4j library — which was embedded in thousands of enterprise products. Mitigation: SCA (Software Composition Analysis) tools in CI/CD pipelines, automated dependency updates, SBOM (Software Bill of Materials) for all applications.

A07: Identification and Authentication Failures — weak password policies, missing MFA, credential stuffing susceptibility (no rate limiting on login endpoints), insecure session management (session tokens not invalidated on logout). Mitigation: enforce strong passwords, require MFA for all accounts, implement account lockout and rate limiting, use secure session token generation and storage.

A08: Software and Data Integrity Failures — includes both insecure deserialization (deserializing untrusted data can lead to remote code execution) and CI/CD pipeline integrity failures (malicious code injected into the build pipeline). The SolarWinds supply chain attack exploited a CI/CD pipeline integrity failure. Mitigation: verify digital signatures on updates and packages, implement supply chain security controls, use SLSA framework for build provenance.

A09: Security Logging and Monitoring Failures — without comprehensive logging, attackers can operate in your environment for months undetected. The average breach dwell time (time from initial compromise to detection) was 194 days in 2025. Mitigation: log all authentication events, access control failures, and input validation failures; centralize logs in a SIEM; set up alerting on anomalous patterns; test detection capabilities regularly.

A10: Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) — the server makes HTTP requests on behalf of an attacker-controlled input, potentially reaching internal services not accessible from the internet. AWS EC2 instance metadata service (IMDSv1) was particularly vulnerable to SSRF — an attacker could make the application server request http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/ to retrieve the EC2 instance''s IAM credentials. Mitigation: validate and sanitize all URLs before making server-side requests, use allowlists of permitted destinations, block access to internal metadata endpoints.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 1;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Secure SDLC integrates security into every phase of development. Learn security requirements, secure design patterns, code review methodology, and BSIMM and OWASP SAMM maturity models.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Secure Software Development Lifecycle (SSDLC) — Security by Design',
      'description', 'Secure SDLC integrates security into every phase of development. Learn security requirements, secure design patterns, code review methodology, and BSIMM and OWASP SAMM maturity models.',
      'transcript', 'The Secure Software Development Lifecycle (SSDLC) is the practice of integrating security activities into every phase of software development — from initial requirements through architecture, development, testing, deployment, and operations. The alternative — adding security at the end of development as a final gate before release — is both less effective and far more expensive.

The security cost curve is the most important argument for SSDLC investment. Research from IBM and NIST consistently shows that fixing a security defect in requirements costs $1 in effort; the same defect found in design costs $6; in development costs $10; in testing costs $15; post-release costs $100. The math is unambiguous: security investment in early phases has dramatically higher ROI than finding the same defect in production.

Security requirements are the foundation. Before writing a line of code, ask: what security properties must this application have? Authentication requirements (which users, what MFA level, what session timeout?), authorization requirements (what roles exist, what can each role do?), data sensitivity requirements (what data is processed, how must it be protected?), logging requirements (what events must be logged for audit and incident response?), third-party integration requirements (what security controls must third-party APIs meet?). Security requirements derived from business requirements, regulatory obligations (GDPR, HIPAA, PCI DSS), and threat modeling prevent entire vulnerability classes by design.

Threat modeling in the design phase is the highest-leverage security activity in the SDLC. A two-hour threat modeling session for a new feature identifies architectural flaws before any code is written. Process: draw a data flow diagram, apply STRIDE to each component and data flow, identify missing controls, add security requirements for each identified threat. Output: an updated architecture with security controls designed in, not bolted on.

Secure coding standards define what "correct" code looks like for security-sensitive operations. Language-specific standards (OWASP''s Secure Coding Practices, CERT C/C++ Coding Standards, Google''s Java Security Best Practices) cover input validation, output encoding, authentication, cryptography, error handling, and logging. Code review for security should be separate from functional code review: security-focused reviewers look specifically for vulnerability patterns rather than logic correctness. Key patterns to review: every point where user input touches SQL queries or system commands, every authentication and authorization check, every cryptographic operation, and every error handling block.

BSIMM (Building Security In Maturity Model) is an empirical study of real-world software security programs, updated annually by observing 130+ participating organizations. BSIMM defines 119 software security activities across 12 practices in 4 domains: Governance (strategy, compliance, training), Intelligence (attack models, security features/design, standards), SSDLC Touchpoints (architecture analysis, code review, security testing), and Deployment (penetration testing, software environment, configuration management). BSIMM lets you benchmark your program''s maturity against your industry peer group.

OWASP SAMM (Software Assurance Maturity Model) provides a prescriptive roadmap for building an AppSec program from Level 0 (no security activity) to Level 3 (optimized, proactive security program). SAMM assesses 15 security practices across 5 business functions: Governance (strategy & metrics, policy & compliance, education & guidance), Design (threat assessment, security requirements, security architecture), Implementation (secure build, secure deployment, defect management), Verification (architecture assessment, requirements-driven testing, security testing), and Operations (incident management, environment management, operational management).'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 2;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'SAST, DAST, and SCA are the three pillars of automated application security testing. Learn to integrate them into CI/CD pipelines, tune for low false positive rates, and build a security gate that does not slow delivery.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'SAST, DAST & SCA — Automated Security Testing in the Pipeline',
      'description', 'SAST, DAST, and SCA are the three pillars of automated application security testing. Learn to integrate them into CI/CD pipelines, tune for low false positive rates, and build a security gate that does not slow delivery.',
      'transcript', 'Automated application security testing brings security to every developer''s pull request rather than relying on periodic manual assessments. Three complementary techniques cover different aspects of the application attack surface: SAST examines source code, DAST attacks the running application, and SCA audits third-party dependencies.

SAST (Static Application Security Testing) analyzes source code or bytecode without executing it. It can find: SQL injection patterns (string concatenation used to build SQL queries), hardcoded credentials (API keys, database passwords in source code — an extraordinarily common finding in real-world code reviews), insecure cryptographic practices (use of MD5 for password hashing), path traversal vulnerabilities, and taint analysis (tracking user-controlled data from input to dangerous sinks). SAST runs at build time and integrates directly into IDEs for developer feedback during coding.

Major SAST tools: Checkmarx SAST (enterprise-grade, supports 25+ languages, strong taint analysis, integrates with major CI/CD platforms), Semgrep (open-source core, highly customizable rule writing in YAML, extremely fast — can scan a large codebase in seconds, popular for writing custom rules for organization-specific security patterns), SonarQube (broad language support, combines security, quality, and coverage analysis — useful as a developer-facing quality gate). GitHub Advanced Security (CodeQL) is GitHub''s native SAST, available free for public repositories.

SAST limitation: high false positive rates without tuning. A fresh SAST deployment on a large codebase will surface hundreds or thousands of findings, the majority of which are false positives (code patterns that look vulnerable but are not, due to context the tool cannot analyze). Tuning SAST for production use requires: defining a scope (focus on high-risk vulnerability classes relevant to your technology stack), suppressing known false positives (with documented justification), and setting actionable thresholds (fail the build only on Critical and High findings, not all findings).

DAST (Dynamic Application Security Testing) attacks the running application from the outside — the same perspective as an external attacker. DAST tools send HTTP requests containing attack payloads and analyze responses for vulnerability indicators. DAST can find: XSS (injects script payloads and detects reflected output), SQL injection (injects SQL syntax and detects database errors or behavioral differences), authentication weaknesses (tries common passwords, tests session management), and SSRF (injects internal URLs and checks for response content).

OWASP ZAP (Zed Attack Proxy) is the open-source DAST standard, widely used in CI/CD pipelines. Burp Suite Professional is the premium DAST platform preferred for manual testing and deeper assessment. DAST runs in a staging or test environment (never in production — it sends attack payloads), and requires an authenticated session (either pre-configured login credentials or an authenticated proxy recording) to test authorized functionality.

SCA (Software Composition Analysis) identifies vulnerabilities in open-source dependencies — the libraries, frameworks, and packages that make up 70–90% of modern application code. Snyk is the market leader: integrates with GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, npm, Maven, PyPI, NuGet, and Docker, providing automated pull requests to upgrade vulnerable dependencies. Dependabot (GitHub-native) automatically creates PRs to update dependencies with known CVEs. SCA findings should be enriched with reachability analysis (is the vulnerable function in this library actually called by your application? If not, the effective risk is lower) and fix availability (is a patched version available, and what is the upgrade path complexity?).'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 3;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'APIs are the largest and fastest-growing attack surface in enterprise environments. Master OWASP API Security Top 10, OAuth 2.0 flows, JWT hardening, and API gateway security architecture.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'API Security — OWASP API Top 10, Authentication & Authorization Flaws',
      'description', 'APIs are the largest and fastest-growing attack surface in enterprise environments. Master OWASP API Security Top 10, OAuth 2.0 flows, JWT hardening, and API gateway security architecture.',
      'transcript', 'APIs (Application Programming Interfaces) have become the connective tissue of the modern enterprise — every mobile app, SaaS integration, microservices architecture, and third-party data exchange uses APIs. They are also one of the fastest-growing attack surfaces: the average enterprise has 613 API endpoints, many undocumented, and API attacks grew 400% between 2020 and 2025. OWASP publishes a dedicated API Security Top 10 to address the unique vulnerability patterns of APIs.

API1: Broken Object Level Authorization (BOLA) — the most common and most impactful API vulnerability. Occurs when an API endpoint takes an object identifier from user input (user ID, order ID, document ID) and retrieves that object without verifying that the requesting user is authorized to access it. Example: GET /api/v1/orders/7834 returns order 7834 regardless of whether the authenticated user owns that order. Attackers systematically enumerate IDs (7834, 7835, 7836...) to access every object in the system. Mitigation: validate authorization on every object access — does the authenticated user have permission to access this specific object? Never assume authorization at the endpoint level covers object-level access.

API2: Broken Authentication — weak or missing authentication on API endpoints. Common issues: API keys transmitted in URL parameters (logged in proxy and browser history), long-lived API tokens with no expiration, no rate limiting on authentication endpoints (enabling credential stuffing), missing token revocation (tokens remain valid after logout), and JWT misconfigurations (none algorithm attack, weak signing keys, missing signature verification).

API5: Broken Function Level Authorization — higher-privilege API functions accessible to lower-privilege users. Example: DELETE /api/v1/users/{id} is accessible to regular users, not just administrators. Mitigation: explicit authorization checks for every function, not just at the endpoint level.

OAuth 2.0 is the authorization framework that enables delegated access — allowing a third-party application to access resources on behalf of a user, without the user sharing their credentials with the third party. Four grant types: Authorization Code (most secure — user authenticates with the authorization server, which returns a one-time code, exchanged for an access token — used for web applications), Authorization Code with PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange — extends Authorization Code flow for public clients like mobile apps that cannot securely store a client secret), Client Credentials (machine-to-machine — the client authenticates directly with no user involvement), Device Flow (for input-constrained devices like smart TVs). Authorization Code flow is the correct choice for user-delegated access in web applications. Never use the implicit flow (deprecated in OAuth 2.1) which returns tokens in URL fragments.

JWT (JSON Web Token) is the most common format for OAuth 2.0 access tokens and OIDC identity tokens. Structure: Base64-encoded header (algorithm and token type) + Base64-encoded payload (claims: user ID, roles, expiration, issuer) + signature (HMAC-SHA256 or RSA signature over header + payload). JWT security considerations: verify the signature on every request (NEVER trust claims without signature verification), validate the algorithm (reject "none" algorithm — a known attack), use asymmetric signing (RS256) rather than symmetric (HS256) when tokens are validated by multiple services (each service only needs the public key, not the secret), set short expiration times (15 minutes for access tokens, with refresh tokens for longer sessions), include only necessary claims (avoid embedding sensitive data in JWTs — they are signed but not encrypted).'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 4;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'DevSecOps shifts security left into every pull request. Learn to build security gates in CI/CD pipelines, implement secrets scanning, IaC security checks, and runtime protection without slowing developer velocity.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'DevSecOps — Shifting Security Left in CI/CD Pipelines',
      'description', 'DevSecOps shifts security left into every pull request. Learn to build security gates in CI/CD pipelines, implement secrets scanning, IaC security checks, and runtime protection without slowing developer velocity.',
      'transcript', 'DevSecOps is the philosophy and practice of integrating security into the software development and delivery pipeline so that every code change is automatically assessed for security issues before it reaches production. The goal is not to slow down development — it is to catch security issues when they are cheapest to fix, before they are deployed.

The DevSecOps pipeline has security gates at every stage. Pre-commit: developer-side hooks that run before code is committed to version control. Gitleaks and TruffleHog scan for secrets (API keys, passwords, certificates) embedded in code changes. This is the first and most efficient defense against secrets leakage — stopping a secret at commit time prevents it from ever entering the repository history. Run-time: a few seconds, no pipeline latency.

Commit/build stage: when code is pushed and the CI pipeline starts. SAST runs on the new code (Semgrep for custom rules, CodeQL for deep taint analysis). SCA runs on dependency files (package.json, requirements.txt, pom.xml) to detect newly introduced vulnerable dependencies. Container image builds trigger Trivy or Grype scanning of the resulting image. License scanning checks for open-source licenses incompatible with commercial distribution. Gate criterion: pipeline fails (blocks merge to main) on Critical and High security findings. Development teams are notified immediately with the specific finding and remediation guidance.

Deploy stage: Infrastructure as Code scanning runs before any infrastructure changes are applied. Checkov (supports Terraform, CloudFormation, Kubernetes manifests, Helm charts, Dockerfiles) checks IaC configurations against security best practices and compliance frameworks — catching "open security group allowing all inbound traffic on port 22" before the Terraform plan is applied. Tfsec is specifically optimized for Terraform. Terrascan supports OPA (Open Policy Agent) for custom policy rules. Gate criterion: block deployment on critical IaC security misconfigurations.

Staging/test stage: DAST runs against the deployed application in a staging environment. Automated API security testing using OWASP ZAP or Nuclei with application-specific templates. Dependency vulnerability scanning against the running application to verify all SCA findings were resolved before staging deployment.

Production runtime: WAF (Web Application Firewall) inspects and filters HTTP traffic at the edge. RASP (Runtime Application Self-Protection) instruments the application itself to detect and block attacks in real time — a RASP agent inside the application detects a SQL injection payload before it reaches the database query, regardless of whether that specific payload was in the WAF ruleset. Runtime container security (Falco) monitors container behavior and alerts on unexpected activity.

Security as code means security policies are defined in machine-readable formats and enforced programmatically — not through manual processes. OPA (Open Policy Agent) with Rego language defines organizational security policies: Kubernetes admission policies (no pods running as root), Terraform validation policies (no public S3 buckets), and API authorization policies (RBAC decisions as code). Policies checked into version control, reviewed like code, and enforced in the pipeline create a auditable, testable, and scalable security governance system.

Developer enablement is the cultural component of DevSecOps. Security gates that block development without providing actionable guidance create frustration, workarounds, and adversarial relationships between security and development teams. Effective DevSecOps provides: specific remediation guidance with every finding (not just "SQL injection found" but "use parameterized queries — here is the corrected code snippet"), security office hours for developers to ask questions, a fast exception process for false positives, and security champions within each development team who are empowered to make basic security decisions without waiting for the central security team.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 5;

-- ─── MODULE 6: Digital Forensics & Incident Response (DFIR) ──────────────────

  SELECT id INTO v_ch FROM public.chapters
    WHERE course_id = v_cid ORDER BY order_index LIMIT 1 OFFSET 5;
  IF v_ch IS NULL THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'M06 chapter not found'; END IF;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'DFIR starts long before an incident occurs. Learn evidence handling procedures, chain of custody requirements, legal admissibility standards, and the order of volatility that governs which evidence to collect first.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'DFIR Foundations — Evidence Handling, Chain of Custody & Legal Framework',
      'description', 'DFIR starts long before an incident occurs. Learn evidence handling procedures, chain of custody requirements, legal admissibility standards, and the order of volatility that governs which evidence to collect first.',
      'transcript', 'Digital forensics is the science of collecting, preserving, analyzing, and presenting digital evidence in a manner that is legally defensible. Incident response is the operational process of containing and eradicating a security incident. Together as DFIR, these disciplines determine whether an organization can understand what happened in a breach, attribute it to a threat actor, support criminal prosecution or civil litigation, and prevent recurrence.

Evidence handling begins with a fundamental principle: protect the integrity of evidence above all else. Any action that modifies the original evidence — however well-intentioned — risks making that evidence inadmissible in legal proceedings and undermines the credibility of your entire investigation. The golden rule of digital forensics: never work on the original. Always create a forensic copy first, verify the copy''s integrity, and conduct all analysis on the copy.

Chain of custody is the documented record of every person who has handled a piece of evidence, when they received it, what they did with it, and when they passed it on. Chain of custody documentation must be unbroken from collection to courtroom. Any gap in the chain — an unlogged transfer, an unlabeled evidence bag, a period where custody is unclear — provides defense attorneys an opportunity to challenge the evidence''s integrity and admissibility. Chain of custody log entries must include: unique evidence identifier, description of the item (make/model/serial for hardware, file name/hash for digital files), date and time of collection, collector''s name and signature, condition of item at collection, every subsequent transfer (from/to/date/time/reason), and storage location at each stage.

Order of volatility defines which evidence to collect first based on how quickly it will be lost. RFC 3227 (Guidelines for Evidence Collection and Archiving) codifies the order: CPU registers and cache (lost immediately on power cycle), RAM and running processes (lost on shutdown — must be captured live), network state and connections (changes continuously), running process list (changes continuously), temporary file system contents, disk storage (persistent — survives power cycle), remote logs (may be overwritten based on rotation policy), archived media (most persistent). The order of volatility determines triage decisions in the first minutes of an incident: before pulling the power on a compromised server, capture the RAM — it may contain encryption keys, running malware artifacts, or attacker commands that will be lost the moment power is cut.

The legal framework for digital forensics varies by jurisdiction. In US federal cases: Fourth Amendment governs searches and seizures — evidence obtained without proper authorization may be excluded (Fourth Amendment applies to government actors; private employers conducting internal investigations have broader latitude but must comply with employment law). Computer Fraud and Abuse Act (CFAA) defines the legal boundaries of computer access investigations. UK: Police and Criminal Evidence Act (PACE) governs evidence admissibility. EU: GDPR implications for personal data collected during forensic investigations — forensic evidence containing personal data must be handled in compliance with GDPR even when collected for security purposes.

DFIR preparation: the organizations with the best outcomes in major incidents are those that prepared before the incident occurred. Preparation activities: establish the DFIR team with clearly defined roles (incident commander, forensic lead, communications lead, legal liaison), maintain pre-authorized forensic tools on a dedicated forensic workstation (write blockers, FTK Imager, Volatility3, Wireshark, Autopsy), document forensic procedures and chain of custody forms in advance, maintain relationships with outside forensic counsel and external IR firms for surge support, and run tabletop exercises that include evidence preservation scenarios. The first 30 minutes of a major incident determine the quality of forensic evidence available for the entire investigation.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 1;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Disk forensics is the art of recovering what attackers tried to hide. Learn forensic imaging, file system artifact analysis, deleted file recovery, Windows Registry forensics, and Linux artifact locations.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Disk Forensics — Acquisition, File System Analysis & Artifact Recovery',
      'description', 'Disk forensics is the art of recovering what attackers tried to hide. Learn forensic imaging, file system acquisition, deleted file recovery, Windows Registry forensics, and Linux artifact locations.',
      'transcript', 'Disk forensics examines the stored contents of digital media — hard drives, SSDs, USB devices, and their forensic images — to reconstruct what happened on a system, recover deleted evidence, and identify attacker activity. Even when attackers attempt to cover their tracks, file systems leave forensic traces that trained examiners can recover.

Forensic acquisition creates an exact bit-for-bit copy of the source media, including deleted files, unallocated space, and slack space (the unused portion of the last cluster in a file). Tools: FTK Imager (Windows, free, widely accepted in legal proceedings), dc3dd (Linux, built on dd with verification and logging), Guymager (Linux GUI, fast, generates verification hashes automatically). Write blockers are hardware or software devices that allow the forensic tool to read from the source media while physically preventing any writes — essential for preserving the original evidence. After acquisition, generate and record a cryptographic hash (MD5 and SHA-256) of the forensic image and the original — if these hashes match at any point in the investigation, you have proof the image is an unmodified copy of the original.

NTFS (the Windows file system) stores rich forensic information in its Master File Table (MFT). The MFT contains a record for every file and directory on the volume, including: file name, creation time, modified time, accessed time, MFT record change time, file size, and the data runs (disk clusters) where the file''s content is stored. Critically, NTFS metadata retains records of files even after they are deleted (until the MFT record is overwritten). Tools: MFTECmd (Eric Zimmerman, free) parses the MFT into CSV output for timeline analysis.

Windows forensic artifacts: shellbag artifacts (Windows Registry keys that record which folders a user accessed via Explorer — useful for proving a user accessed specific directories), LNK files (shortcut files created automatically when a user opens a file — contain the full path of the opened file and timestamps, proving access to files that may have been deleted), prefetch files (Windows caches information about the first 128 applications executed — prefetch entries prove a program was executed even if it has since been deleted), Windows Registry (hive files at C:\Windows\System32\config\ — SYSTEM, SECURITY, SAM, SOFTWARE hives record installed software, USB device history, recent network connections, user account information, and auto-run persistence mechanisms), browser history (Chrome stores history in SQLite databases in the user profile — LastVisitTime, URL, visit count, and referrer chain), Windows Event Logs (Security event log — 4624 successful logon, 4625 failed logon, 4688 process creation, 4740 account lockout — in EVTX format, parsed by EvtxECmd or Windows Event Viewer).

Linux forensic artifacts: /var/log/ directory (auth.log/secure — authentication events, syslog — system events, audit/audit.log — auditd syscall logging if enabled, apache2/access.log — web server access), bash history (~/.bash_history — command history, but easily cleared by attacker), /etc/passwd and /etc/shadow (user accounts and password hashes), cron jobs (/etc/cron* directories and user crontabs — common persistence mechanism for malware), systemd services (/etc/systemd/system/ — can be used for persistence), /tmp and /dev/shm (commonly used by malware for temporary files — these directories are often executable and world-writable), and the Linux audit subsystem (auditd with appropriate rules configured before the incident provides syscall-level logging of every file access, process execution, and network connection).

SSD forensics presents unique challenges. Unlike HDDs where deleted file content remains in unallocated space until overwritten, SSDs use wear-leveling algorithms and TRIM (which instructs the SSD to zero deleted data blocks for performance) that can rapidly eliminate deleted file content. SSD forensics requires immediate acquisition before TRIM operates and may require specialized hardware to bypass SSD encryption.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 2;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Memory forensics recovers attacker tools that never touch the disk. Learn Volatility3, process injection detection, credential extraction from LSASS, and live memory acquisition techniques.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Memory Forensics — Live Analysis, Process Injection & Malware Detection',
      'description', 'Memory forensics recovers attacker tools that never touch the disk. Learn Volatility3, process injection detection, credential extraction from LSASS, and live memory acquisition techniques.',
      'transcript', 'Memory forensics is the analysis of a system''s RAM to identify running malware, extract attacker credentials, and recover evidence that never existed on disk. It has become one of the most critical DFIR skills because sophisticated attackers specifically design their tools to avoid disk-based detection — running entirely in memory, injecting into legitimate processes, and leaving no files for antivirus to scan.

Memory acquisition captures the current state of RAM. On live systems: winpmem (Windows, free, maintained by Velocidex) and LiME (Linux Memory Extractor, a kernel module that creates a raw memory dump). On virtual machines: suspend the VM and export the memory snapshot file (VMware .vmem, Hyper-V .bin, VirtualBox .sav) — this is forensically preferable because it is atomic and does not require installing any tools on the target system. Memory acquisition is time-critical: RAM is volatile by definition. Every minute that passes after compromise, attacker-injected code may execute additional actions, and the memory state changes. On a suspected compromised system, memory capture is the first forensic action.

Volatility3 is the universal memory forensics framework. It supports Windows, Linux, and macOS memory images and provides dozens of plugins for different analysis tasks. Key plugins: pslist (list running processes — basic, shows only active processes in the process list), pstree (show process hierarchy — reveals parent-child relationships that expose unusual process spawns, e.g., WinWord.exe spawning PowerShell.exe), psscan (scan memory for EPROCESS structures — finds hidden processes that rootkits have unlinked from the process list but that still exist in memory), cmdline (show command-line arguments for each process — reveals what commands were executed and with what arguments), netscan (network connections — which processes have open network connections, to which remote IPs and ports), filescan (scan memory for file objects — can recover references to files that have been deleted from disk), dumpfiles (extract file contents from memory — recover executables or documents that were open at the time of capture).

Process injection is the technique of inserting malicious code into the memory space of a legitimate process to disguise activity. Detection with Volatility3: malfind plugin scans process memory for regions marked as executable that are not mapped to a file on disk (legitimate code is almost always backed by a DLL or EXE on disk; code injected directly into memory has no file backing). Injection techniques: DLL injection (attacker writes a DLL path into the target process''s memory and triggers LoadLibrary), process hollowing (attacker creates a legitimate process in suspended state, replaces its memory contents with malicious code, and resumes execution — the process list shows a legitimate process name running malicious code), reflective DLL injection (malicious DLL includes its own loader, injected directly as shellcode without touching disk), and process doppelganging (uses Windows TxF transactional file system to create a process from a file that exists only in a transaction, never committed to disk).

LSASS (Local Security Authority Subsystem Service) is the Windows process responsible for authentication — it holds credentials in memory for currently logged-in users, including NTLM hashes, Kerberos tickets, and (in some configurations) cleartext passwords. Dumping LSASS memory is the most common credential harvesting technique: attackers use MiniDumpWriteDump (ProcDump, Task Manager, or custom tools) to create a process dump file, then use Mimikatz locally to extract credentials from the dump. Detection: Windows Defender Credential Guard virtualizes LSASS and prevents direct memory reading. Windows Event 10 (process access to LSASS) in Sysmon logging alerts on credential dumping attempts. Memory forensics: Volatility3''s lsadump plugin extracts cached credentials from the memory image without needing to run Mimikatz.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 3;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Network forensics reconstructs attacker communications from packet captures and flow data. Learn Wireshark analysis, C2 traffic detection, data exfiltration patterns, and network timeline reconstruction.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Network Forensics — Packet Capture, Flow Analysis & C2 Detection',
      'description', 'Network forensics reconstructs attacker communications from packet captures and flow data. Learn Wireshark analysis, C2 traffic detection, data exfiltration patterns, and network timeline reconstruction.',
      'transcript', 'Network forensics examines network traffic — both full packet captures and flow records — to reconstruct attacker communications, identify lateral movement, detect data exfiltration, and build a timeline of the attack. Even when attackers clear logs on compromised hosts, network-level evidence frequently survives because it is recorded on network infrastructure the attacker does not control.

Network forensic data sources: full packet capture (PCAP) — every byte of every packet, the richest data source but expensive to store at enterprise scale. NetFlow/IPFIX — flow records summarize connections (source IP, destination IP, source port, destination port, protocol, bytes transferred, packets, start time, end time) without capturing payload content. NetFlow is 100–1000x smaller than full PCAP for the same traffic volume. DNS logs — every DNS query made by hosts on the network, with timestamp and resolution result. Proxy logs — for HTTP/HTTPS traffic routed through a proxy, full URL requested, response code, bytes transferred, and user agent. Firewall logs — connection allowed/denied records with 5-tuple (src IP, dst IP, src port, dst port, protocol).

Wireshark is the universal packet analysis tool. Key analysis techniques for DFIR: display filters to isolate relevant traffic (ip.addr == 10.0.0.50 && tcp.port == 443), following TCP streams to read the full conversation of a TCP connection (right-click → Follow → TCP Stream), extracting files transferred over HTTP (File → Export Objects → HTTP), and analyzing TLS handshakes to identify certificate details and protocol versions of encrypted connections. Time display format matters: set absolute timestamps (Edit → Preferences → Columns) when building an incident timeline, not relative timestamps.

Command-and-control (C2) traffic detection: attackers establish C2 channels to maintain access to compromised hosts and receive commands. Modern C2 frameworks (Cobalt Strike, Sliver, Havoc) are designed to evade detection by mimicking legitimate traffic patterns. Detection indicators: beaconing patterns (regular, periodic connections to an external IP or domain — e.g., a connection every 60 seconds with ±10% jitter, exactly as Cobalt Strike''s default profile specifies), domain generation algorithms (malware generates thousands of random domain names and registers a small number of them — DNS traffic shows systematic queries to random-looking domains), long-duration connections (a single HTTP connection held open for hours, used as a C2 channel), DNS tunneling (large TXT record queries, high query frequency to a single domain — Iodine and dnscat2 exfiltrate data through DNS), and HTTPS to unusual certificates (self-signed certificates, recently registered domains, or domains with no web presence outside the suspected C2 channel).

Data exfiltration patterns: large outbound data transfers (NetFlow records show megabytes or gigabytes of outbound traffic to external IP addresses, particularly during off-hours), uploads to cloud storage (connections to Dropbox, Google Drive, OneDrive, or personal file-sharing services from a production server), compressed archive transfers (large single files — ZIP, RAR, 7z — transferred outbound from a sensitive data server), and slow-and-low exfiltration (small amounts of data transferred repeatedly over days or weeks to avoid triggering volume-based thresholds).

Lateral movement detection in network forensics: SMB traffic between workstations (in a properly segmented network, workstations should not initiate SMB connections to each other — internal SMB scanning indicates reconnaissance or Pass-the-Hash lateral movement), RDP connections originating from unexpected hosts (a user workstation initiating RDP to servers it normally does not connect to), WMI remote execution (DCOM traffic on port 135 followed by dynamic high-port connections from unexpected source hosts), and authentication traffic patterns (Kerberos TGS requests for multiple services from a single host in rapid succession indicates credential-based reconnaissance or Kerberoasting).'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 4;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Malware analysis reveals attacker tools and techniques. Master static analysis, dynamic sandbox execution, YARA rule writing, and producing a DFIR report that stands up to legal and regulatory scrutiny.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Malware Analysis Fundamentals & DFIR Report Writing',
      'description', 'Malware analysis reveals attacker tools and techniques. Master static analysis, dynamic sandbox execution, YARA rule writing, and producing a DFIR report that stands up to legal and regulatory scrutiny.',
      'transcript', 'Malware analysis is the process of reverse-engineering malicious software to understand its capabilities, identify its origin, and develop detection signatures. For DFIR practitioners, malware analysis answers the questions that incident response depends on: what did this malware do? what data did it access or exfiltrate? how did it persist? what other systems did it communicate with? what additional malware did it drop?

Static analysis examines a malware sample without executing it. First step: file identification and hashing. Generate MD5, SHA1, and SHA256 hashes and check against threat intelligence databases — VirusTotal aggregates results from 70+ antivirus engines and tells you immediately if a sample is known malware and what family it belongs to. File type identification: many malware samples disguise themselves with misleading extensions — use the file magic bytes (first few bytes of the file) rather than the extension to determine the true file type. Strings extraction: the strings command (or FLOSS for obfuscated strings) extracts human-readable text from the binary — URLs (C2 domains), IP addresses, registry keys, file paths, and error messages embedded in the binary reveal its capabilities and infrastructure without execution.

PE (Portable Executable) header analysis for Windows malware: import table lists every Windows API function the binary imports — a binary importing CreateRemoteThread, VirtualAllocEx, and WriteProcessMemory is almost certainly performing process injection. Section analysis: .text (executable code), .data (global variables), .rsrc (resources — embedded files). Packed malware has high entropy (random-looking data, characteristic of compression/encryption) and minimal imports (only the loader code, which unpacks the real malware at runtime). Tools: PEiD (packer identification), PE-bear (PE structure viewer), pestudio (combines multiple static analysis tools into one GUI).

YARA is the pattern-matching language for malware detection. YARA rules define conditions that match malware samples based on byte sequences, string patterns, file structure characteristics, or a combination. Example: a YARA rule that matches all samples that contain the string "cmd.exe /c whoami" AND have PE section entropy above 7.0 AND import CreateRemoteThread. YARA rules can be deployed in antivirus products, EDR tools, SIEM log detection, network intrusion detection systems, and forensic tools to identify malware samples across all of these detection surfaces simultaneously.

Dynamic analysis executes the malware in a controlled sandbox environment and observes its behavior. Cuckoo Sandbox (open-source) and commercial services (Any.run, JoeSandbox, Hybrid Analysis) provide isolated Windows or Linux VMs where malware can be executed safely. Sandbox analysis reports: all processes created (process tree), all files created or modified, all registry keys read or written, all network connections (DNS queries, HTTP requests, raw TCP/UDP connections), screenshots of the desktop at intervals (for ransomware, shows the encryption progress and ransom note), and memory dumps of suspicious processes. Limitations: sophisticated malware detects sandbox environments (checks for VM artifacts, user interaction, internet connectivity) and alters behavior or does not execute. Dynamic analysis should follow static analysis so you know what sandbox evasion techniques to anticipate.

A DFIR report must be factual, precise, and legally defensible. Structure: Executive Summary (what happened, when, what was affected, what has been done to contain and remediate — written for non-technical leadership), Incident Timeline (every confirmed event with timestamp, data source, and confidence level — distinguish between confirmed facts, high-confidence assessments, and working hypotheses), Technical Findings (detailed analysis of each compromised system, malware analyzed, and evidence collected), Evidence Index (complete list of all evidence collected with hash values, collection dates, and custody records), Root Cause Analysis (how did the attacker initially gain access? what controls failed?), Containment Actions Taken (what was done to stop ongoing harm?), Remediation Recommendations (what controls need to be added or improved to prevent recurrence?), and Appendices (raw evidence extracts, tool outputs, malware analysis reports). Every factual claim must cite the specific evidence that supports it — assertions without evidence documentation cannot withstand legal or regulatory scrutiny.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 5;

END $$;

